In an early study with Gaussian noise added to QM9 labels, the published methods for noisy labels that compared molecules by a distance did worst. We hypothesized that the distance was at fault. On a given dataset, most features of a general-purpose representation either do not vary or do not relate to the property. Yet each adds a term to the distance, so together they dominate it (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}). We propose instead to compute distances over a hybrid representation: a fixed number of task-relevant features selected from several representations. A first version showed that the selected features work as ordinary model inputs too, so the question became how to choose them (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}). Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} tests whether distance in the hybrid tracks the label better, and Chapter~\ref{chap:noise} returns to noisy labels, comparing the single representations with the hybrid. The same problem appears in antibody representations, where the features that matter are few and buried in representations of the whole sequence. For this reason we test the method on antibodies, and on chemical reactions.

The second half asks whether the representation itself can be adapted to the task:\label{sec:intro:hybrid_questions}
