\section{Outline of the thesis and contributions}

Chapter~\ref{chap:background} introduces molecular representations, model families, molecular similarity and activity cliffs, and the sources of noise in molecular data. It defines the seven noise conditions used in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} and the three groups of methods for noisy labels (noise detection, noise mitigation and noise-robust training), and closes with artificial noise injection, and evaluation metrics.

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: broken last sentence; "variational head" models named as in Chapter 3
Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} looks into what makes a QSAR model robust to label noise. The primary prediction target is the HOMO--LUMO gap in QM9, whose computed labels carry negligible measurement noise. The same experiments were also run on three assay datasets: LogD, Caco-2 and hERG $K_i$. We compare seven different types of label noise, referred to as noise conditions. We matched the quantity of noise to the training set label spread for all but one of the noise conditions. We measure noise robustness as the proportion of predictive performance retained as artificial label noise increases. We found that model family was the main factor in noise robustness, and choice of molecular representation matters more for predictive performance than noise robustness. Strong predictive performance on clean labels does not imply robustness. The models with the highest predictive performance, LightGBM, XGBoost and the plain neural networks lost the most with increasing label noise, while random forests were the most consistent model across datasets, representations, and noise conditions. We replicated systematic noise by adding it in disproportionate amounts to select scaffold families and by clipping labels at a made-up assay limit. Models struggled the most when training on this systematic noise. Label noise raised mean predicted uncertainty for the probabilistic models which estimate it; however, only the BNNs with a variance head, the heteroscedastic GP and the heteroscedastic VBLL networks correctly attributed the rise in uncertainty to the data rather than to the model itself.

