A predictor has two parts, the representation and the model. The representation also shapes what counts as similar, since similarity depends on both the representation and the coefficient. Activity cliffs and uninformative features, in turn, break the similar property principle. Real assay noise is often neither independent nor identically distributed, and it need not be zero-mean. For this reason we defined seven artificial noise conditions. Methods for noisy labels fall into noise detection, noise mitigation and noise-robust training. Whichever group a method belongs to, none is expected to win on every dataset. We implemented the seven conditions in NoiseInject, an open-source package that injects them and reports the robustness and uncertainty metrics for any model. Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} asks whether the representation or the model architecture governs how strongly a predictor degrades under label noise, through the questions set out in Chapter~\ref{chap:intro}.

