Log $D$ is the logarithm of the distribution coefficient of a molecule between octanol and water, usually measured at pH 7.4. It counts every charge state, whereas log $P$ counts only the neutral form, and a basic amine that is protonated at that pH therefore has a log $D$ below its log $P$. Caco-2 cells come from a human colorectal carcinoma and grow as a layer that models absorption in the gut. Permeability across the layer is measured in both directions, and the efflux ratio is the permeability from the basolateral to the apical side divided by that in the other direction. A ratio above about 2 suggests the compound is pumped out by a transporter such as P-glycoprotein. The hERG gene encodes a cardiac potassium channel. Drugs that block it can prolong the QT interval and cause arrhythmia, so hERG binding is screened early in drug discovery. The hERG $K_i$ dataset holds binding affinities for this channel, as p$K_i$ values.

All splits and CV folds were grouped by the chirality-blind Bemis--Murcko scaffold, implemented such that each distinct acyclic molecule forms its own group, and groups are filled in random order. Prior to modeling, all molecular structures were sanitized and canonicalized using \texttt{RDKit} to remove invalid valence states and standardize atom and bond typing \citep{rdkit}. Prediction targets were standardized to zero mean and unit variance. Experiments that track uncertainty were run once rather than in replicates.

\subsection{Representations}

In this study, we evaluated six molecular representations: extended-connectivity fingerprints of diameter four (ECFP4), Avalon, Sort \& Slice (SNS), physicochemical descriptors (PDV), ChemBERTa and Molecular Hypergraph Grammar GNNs (MHG-GNNs). The same six were used on QM9 and on the three assay datasets. We used the standard ECFP with radius $r = 2$ with $d = 2048$ bits \citep{rogers2010, rdkit}. Sort \& Slice takes the same substructures as ECFP4, but it ranks them by prevalence and selects the top $L$ ($L=1024$) \citep{sns}. We fitted the Sort \& Slice vocabulary on the training molecules alone, refitting it for each QM9 replicate and for each CV fold.  Avalon is a 2,048-bit fingerprint, generated with the Avalon toolkit through RDKit \citep{Gedeck2006, rdkit}. The PDV representation is a vector of 200 physicochemical descriptors computed with RDKit's \texttt{MolecularDescriptorCalculator} \citep{rdkit, Cherkasov2014}; the 200 are listed in Additional file~\ref{app:noise:sec:pdv_descriptors}, and the list is fixed in our code rather than taken from RDKit's default descriptor set, which grows between releases. ChemBERTa embeddings are the mean over the non-padding token embeddings of the \texttt{DeepChem/ChemBERTa-77M-MTR} checkpoint, producing 384 features \citep{Ahmad2022}. MHG-GNNs are a GIN-based autoencoder pre-trained on about 1.38 million PubChem molecules using $\beta$-VAE loss, producing 1024-dimensional embeddings through iterative message passing and found in the published \texttt{mhggnn\_\allowbreak pretrained\_\allowbreak model\_\allowbreak 0724\_\allowbreak 2023} checkpoint \citep{kishimoto2023}. We z-score normalized PDV, ChemBERTa and MHG-GNN per feature, using the training molecules' mean and standard deviation; ECFP4, Avalon and Sort \& Slice reach the model as built.

\subsection{Models}

To understand model behavior in the presence of label noise, we explored a range of model families. We fitted tree ensembles, a support vector machine, Gaussian processes and neural networks. Several are variants of one another, differing in the probabilistic machinery they add, and Additional file~\ref{app:noise:sec:hyperparameters_default} gives every configuration with its settings.

Random Forests (RFs) are one of the most common choices for QSAR modeling thanks to their robustness and interpretability \citep{Svetnik2003}. Quantile Regression Forests (QRFs) hold the distribution of training labels in each leaf and compute quantiles over all trees \citep{Meinshausen2006}. While we used 100 trees for RF, we observed a noisy quantile estimate with 100 trees for QRF, prompting us to use 300 instead. To observe the effect of the quantile mechanism, we then fit a second RF at 300 trees so the two models were identical in all other settings. 

We also used eXtreme Gradient Boosting (XGBoost) \citep{Chen2016}, Light Gradient-Boosting Machine (LightGBM) \citep{ke2017lightgbm}, and Natural Gradient Boosting (NGBoost) \citep{Duan2020}. NGBoost extends deterministic gradient boosting by treating the parameters of a chosen parametric distribution as regression targets and learns them through boosting with a natural gradient update rule \citep{Duan2020}. NGBoost relies on scoring off validation data, which differs by dataset. On QM9 the validation split is already held out of every fit. However, on the three assay datasets the validation set is a scaffold-grouped fifth of the rows NGBoost is handed, which already exclude the validation molecules. 

% TODO: citation check 2026-10-10 -- Svetnik2003 swapped for Cortes2015, which benchmarks an RBF SVM among 12 QSAR algorithms; Svetnik could not be checked for SVMs.
Support vector machines (SVMs) are a well-established baseline in QSAR modeling \citep{Vapnik1995, Cortes2015}. We used a radial basis function (RBF) with $C = 1.0$ and $\gamma = \texttt{scale}$ as the kernel, as it accommodates continuous vectors. 

Gaussian processes (GPs) are a non-parametric class of models that use a kernel to produce a Gaussian predictive distribution over every data point \citep{Obrezanova2007, Rasmussen2005}. We fitted three variants of GPs, each with a constant mean and a scaled kernel. The first uses an RBF kernel and runs on all six representations. The second uses the Tanimoto kernel \citep{Ralaivola2005, moss2020} and was run on ECFP4 alone. The third is a heteroscedastic variant in which the RBF process is given a second network that predicts the observation noise of each molecule. One of the major limitations of using GPs in practice is computational scaling; inference has $O(N^3)$ time complexity and $O(N^2)$ memory, where $N$ is the number of training points \citep{Rasmussen2005}. This can be mitigated with sparse GP approximations, but these introduce approximation error and can be more difficult to tune \citep{quinonero2005}. As such, we used a standard GP and only fit on at most $5{,}000$ training molecules, drawn by a seeded sample. Due to the size of the datasets, this cap only affected QM9. We implemented these GP models in \texttt{GPyTorch} \citep{gpytorch}, with the Tanimoto kernel taken from the \texttt{Gauche} framework \citep{gauche}.

We tested two deterministic feed-forward architectures, both fully connected and both implemented in PyTorch \citep{Paszke2019}: (i) NN-$\alpha$ with two hidden layers of sizes 128 and 64, and dropout after each hidden layer and (ii) NN-$\beta$ with two hidden layers, each of size 128, with dropout applied before the output layer. Each model was trained using the Adam optimizer with early stopping on validation loss. We used Bayesian transformations to convert NNs into Bayesian neural networks (BNNs), and implemented them using \texttt{PyTorch} \citep{Paszke2019} with ReLU activations and dropout regularization ($p=0.2$). Additional file~\ref{app:noise:sec:hyperparameters_default} gives their full widths, dropout fractions, learning rates and batch size.

% TODO(cite): Harrison2024 makes only the last layer Bayesian. It also learns a noise covariance; check the sentence saying VBLL reports only epistemic uncertainty against our implementation.
% TODO: manual review -- fixed: VBLL has one aleatoric variance per fit, not epistemic only
To get these networks producing uncertainty estimates, we applied four probabilistic transformations to each architecture, producing eight probabilistic networks. The first replaces every linear layer with a Bayesian layer carrying a Gaussian prior $\mathcal{N}(0, 0.1^2)$ on its weights, producing BNN-$\alpha$ and BNN-$\beta$. They are trained on an evidence lower bound whose  Kullback–Leibler (KL) divergence term is divided by the number of training molecules. While this is a straightforward approach, it is computationally expensive. We explored alternative techniques like our second variant, where we add an output head to the standard NN and fit the negative log likelihood of a Gaussian with that variance \citep{kendall2017}. The third, Variational Bayesian Last Layer (VBLL), replaces the output layer with a Bayesian last layer that produces mean-field variational posteriors $q(\mathbf{W}) = \mathcal{N}(\boldsymbol{\mu}_W, \text{diag}(\boldsymbol{\sigma}_W^2))$ over the last-layer weights and trains by maximizing the evidence lower bound. This can be thought of as minimizing the reconstruction loss plus KL divergence $D_{\text{KL}}(q(\mathbf{W}) \| p(\mathbf{W}))$ from a standard normal prior, scaled by $1/N$ \citep{Harrison2024}. Because only its output layer keeps a learned observation-noise parameter, VBLL networks report only epistemic uncertainty per molecule, with one aleatoric variance for the whole fit. The fourth makes observation noise a function of the input. All eight probabilistic networks estimate their predictive distributions from 100 stochastic forward passes. Each network and its Bayesian counterpart were tuned to the same set of parameters so that NN-$\alpha$ against BNN-$\alpha$ and NN-$\beta$ against BNN-$\beta$ differ by the Bayesian layers alone. 

