\subsubsection{Per-sample uncertainty and label noise}\label{sec:uq_per_sample}

Finally, we ask whether a model's uncertainty on one molecule tracks the noise on that molecule's label. We assess this using the Spearman correlation between predicted uncertainty and the size of the injected noise over out-of-fold training molecules.

Since Gaussian, Laplace, Student-$t$ and grouped-shifted conditions add noise from the same scale to every label, there is not much for uncertainty to find. It's the same situation with outlier noise, under which a tenth of the data is chosen at random for extra noise, and with grouped-wider in which each scaffold family falls in a single fold, so a model makes predictions based on unseen scaffolds and has no basis for estimating uncertainty. The only case where per-sample uncertainty tracking is possible is censoring, as the noise is dependent on the value of the label itself. The correlations are then large and mostly negative, meaning the model is least uncertain on the labels that were clipped most. On QM9 with half the labels clipped, on PDV, they run from $-0.84$ to $-0.80$ across folds for NGBoost and from $-0.47$ to $-0.41$ for the GP, and on LogD with half the labels clipped every fold of every model except the two GPs is at $-0.29$ or lower. We divided each molecule's error by its uncertainty and found that the division ranked the clipped labels better than the error alone on QM9 and on most of LogD. Models are most confident on the clipped labels, probably because the clipping piles many labels into one value, which the model learns to tightly predict. Ironically, per-molecule uncertainty can be used in reverse to identify clipped labels as the labels a model is more certain about, especially when they fall in a certain range, are most likely to have been clipped.

\section{Conclusions}

We investigated how a variety of molecular representations and models perform under increasing label noise. We used ANOVA variance decomposition across three noise conditions on four datasets to show that molecular representation accounts for a larger share of the variance in predictive performance than in robustness. Robustness to label noise, as measured by \aucnorm, the normalized area under the $R^2$ retention curve, is more impacted by the choice of model. The representation each  model is paired with explains more variance than the representation alone. As such, a representation should not be chosen independently of the model it will be used with, and some models benefit from alternative molecular representations across both predictive performance and noise robustness. Model family was the primary factor in every decomposition except one,  Caco-2 under grouped-shifted noise, where the scaffold fold took the larger share.

Grouped-shifted and censored noise, the two conditions in which the error is systematic, corrupt QSAR model predictions the most. Changing the shape of the distribution from which noise is drawn barely has an impact on model performance, a conclusion which is in agreement with \citet{Heid2023}.
Low quantities of noise do not make much of an impact, on the HOMO--LUMO gap every base model keeps at least 93\% of its clean $R^2$ up to a noise level of 0.3, and grouped-shifted noise separates from the other conditions only from a level of 0.75 (Figure~\ref{fig:conditions}). Models' performance begins to degrade more rapidly on the assay datasets than on the HOMO--LUMO gap, which makes sense as assay data contain inherent experimental noise. Tree and kernel models maintained consistent \aucnorm{} across all representations, while the plain NNs and BNNs depended on the representation they were given, as did the predictive performance of NGBoost. Across datasets and noise conditions, RF and QRF were reliably robust, although their predictive performance on clean labels was middling and on Caco-2 they struggled under Gaussian noise. QRF was the slightly less robust of the two, falling below RF under Gaussian and grouped-shifted noise. Other models performed well only under some conditions. The GP had the highest predictive performance on clean labels and under independent noise but fell behind when the noise was shared within a scaffold family, NGBoost predicted well only with the right representation, and SVM led in predictive performance under scaffold-shared noise on some datasets. Transforming a feed-forward network into a Bayesian one increased \aucnorm, but the Bayesian variants never predicted better at any noise level, so switching to a tree-based model is the stronger move under label noise.

With regard to uncertainty estimation, mean predicted uncertainty rose with label noise for almost every probabilistic model on QM9 and for the majority of models on the assay datasets. Of the models whose aleatoric and epistemic components can be split per molecule, the BNNs with a variance head, the heteroscedastic GP and the heteroscedastic VBLL networks correctly attributed error from the injected noise as aleatoric, while QRF read that error as epistemic. The success of a split is also dependent on the representation, which is not necessarily the case for predictive performance or noise robustness. The aleatoric component rises by roughly 0.4 to 1 unit for each unit of noise added, so it can compare noise between datasets or between noise levels, but it does not measure a dataset's internal error. Outside censoring, no model's per-sample uncertainty identified which labels had been corrupted. Under censoring the models' most confident predictions are the clipped labels themselves, and dividing a molecule's error by its uncertainty picked out the clipped labels better than the error alone.

QM9 served as a relatively clean baseline as its properties are computed. The assay datasets containing experimental measurements on the endpoints LogD, Caco-2 \citep{openadmet}, and hERG $K_i$ \citep{Zdrazil2023} are smaller and cover a more narrow range of the chemical space. As the labels in these data are experimentally-derived and pooled from different laboratories, they contain some unknown amount of noise. We are then adding artificial noise on top of the existing experimental noise. These validation datasets supported the QM9 findings for the forests, with the boosted
trees XGBoost and LightGBM the notable exceptions, degrading on the external datasets. Future work should extend this type of noise robustness benchmarking to classification tasks and larger experimental datasets with better-known uncertainty estimates.

When training on noisy experimental data in the context of QSAR, it is important to keep in mind that a model's robustness to noise is primarily driven by its own mechanism of training, and to an extent the molecular representation the input data comes in, oftentimes with respect to the model. Of the models tested, RF was the most consistent choice across the four datasets, ranking between first and eighth for \aucnorm{} on every dataset, representation and noise condition. Where a predictive distribution is wanted, its probabilistic counterpart QRF maintains RF's predictive performance and nearly all of its noise robustness. What QRF cannot do is say where that uncertainty came from. Beyond the forests, the better choice depends on how much noise there is. LightGBM and XGBoost predict well on clean labels and lose the most as noise rises, so they suit data you trust, while NGBoost and the VBLL networks start lower and hold what they have.

In this research, we define a noise level as a fraction of the spread of the clean training labels. It is therefore relative to the dataset rather than an absolute measurement error, and cannot be mapped onto a given dataset exactly. There are other mechanisms used to estimate the noise in a dataset directly. When an assay's controls are available, the Z$'$-factor can be used to estimate noise \citep{Zhang1999}. You can also use screening data to test for row, column and plate effects, which produce systematic rather than independent error \citep{Caraus2015}. When you lack controls, replicates, and metadata, a label can be checked against the data itself. Confident learning flags labels by comparing each label to what a model predicts for that molecule when it was left out of training \citep{Northcutt2021}. You can use the point at which the model error matches the data's error to generate a predictive limit \citep{Kolmar2021, Crusius2024}. Beyond formal methods, you should consider how a dataset was assembled. How many independent replicates were done, how many laboratories contributed, whether assay protocols were consistently applied, and how varied are the compounds in chemical space?

The first limitation of this study is data availability. While HOMO--LUMO gap from QM9 is a good baseline as its computed labels carry no measurement error, it is only a single task. As the noise is meant to replicate experimental data, this analysis would be improved by more quality-controlled, relatively clean and well-replicated experimental data across different targets. However, data of that nature are hard to find publicly.

Second, \aucnorm{} is a ratio to clean $R^2$, so a model that starts from a low clean baseline has less to lose, and a few replicates scored above 1.05. We tried other measures of noise robustness, but they were either too strongly correlated with clean $R^2$ or did not capture the right quantity. The change in $R^2$ at a single noise level, for example, misses the shape of the curve. \aucnorm{} is not a perfect metric, but it describes the whole curve, and across the 13 base models it is unrelated to clean $R^2$.

Third, it is hard to draw conclusions that hold across so many conditions. No model or representation wins everywhere, although RF comes closest. When working with cheminformatics data, you don't know in advance how a model and representation will perform on the given prediction task. The underlying data is probably noisy, but you don't know how noisy it is or what shape that noise takes.

Given that, our recommendation is to test a handful of models against a handful of representations rather than tuning a single pairing, since on every dataset the pairing explains more of the variance in robustness than the representation does alone. The pattern of the noise should also guide that choice. The same preferences held on all four datasets. Under Laplace, Student-$t$ and outlier noise, RF was the most robust model and SVM was the second. NGBoost lost \aucnorm{} under all three. Under grouped-wider noise, QRF was the most robust model and SVM was close behind. Of the boosted trees, XGBoost lost the most. Grouped-shifted noise was the hardest condition. Every model lost \aucnorm{} on every dataset and representation. RF lost the least of the models that also predicted well, and the GP lost the most. Gaussian noise was the only condition with a clear specialist. NGBoost was the most robust model on the HOMO--LUMO gap under Gaussian noise with every representation, but not on Caco-2 or hERG $K_i$.

These results follow from the loss function each model fits. NGBoost fits a per-molecule scale under a Gaussian likelihood, so it loses ground when the noise is not Gaussian. SVM ignores residuals that fall inside its margin, so it gains under the same conditions. QRF fits conditional quantiles, so a noisy scaffold family widens that family's interval without shifting the fit. Each boosting stage fits the residuals of the stage before it, and those residuals are largest in the noisy families. The GP is the most affected by error that is correlated with structure, because its kernel measures structure. None of this matters if the data are clean, as little separated the models below a noise level of 0.3.

In this chapter each model was given a standard molecular representation, and the choice of representation explained relatively little of the variance in noise robustness. We observe that this choice does impact the performance of different models to varying degrees, and the representations seem to capture different signals on each dataset that we tested. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} asks how well these representations compare molecules on a single dataset.

\chapter{Limitations of the similarity assumption}\label{chap:flaws}

\section{Introduction}

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: abbreviation AC defined at first use
The previous chapters have been focused on the impact of label noise. Before we delve into techniques that are meant to train on, identify, and mitigate such label noise, we first focus on a tangential point, molecular similarity. All of the aforementioned noise-adjacent methods, as well as the QSAR modeling itself, rest on the similar property principle (SPP; Section~\ref{sec:mol_similarity}), which posits that structurally similar compounds display similar chemical properties \citep{Johnson1990}. This serves as the conceptual foundation for QSAR modeling. The concept of molecular similarity is also used in various contexts to compare neighboring molecules. This comes into play when identifying noisy molecules that in theory stick out from their neighbors. There are some important exceptions to the SPP, namely activity cliffs (ACs) \citep{Stumpfe2012, vanTilborg2022}. Furthermore, there is no single way to define similarity between compounds. This is where the principle begins to break down in practice. When defining similarity between two molecules, we typically rely on some pairwise mathematical function, or coefficient, that takes in two molecular representations as input. Two factors vary here, the representation and the coefficient. \citet{Bajusz2015} compared eight coefficients and found Tanimoto, Dice, Cosine and Soergel the best and largely equivalent, while similarities derived from Euclidean and Manhattan distances were not recommended on their own. In this chapter we ask if the standard methods of comparing molecules satisfy the SPP, insofar as they do not sufficiently quantify how similar two molecules actually are.

General-purpose molecular representations are designed to work across chemical domains. Consequently, when considering data from a specific domain, many of their features show little to no variance. Some representations assign separate molecules identical feature vectors. Since common fingerprints do not encode stereochemistry \citep{orsi2024map4c}, they are often unable to differentiate stereoisomers, which can have substantially different measured activity \citep{Nguyen2006}. As these coefficients compare every feature, irrelevant features dilute the result. Different representations also disagree about which molecules are close based on the information they carry. 

% TODO(cite): MODI is a class-weighted ratio, not a plain proportion (Golbraikh2014).
The problem of whether neighboring molecules can predict chemical activity has been measured before. The modelability index of \citet{Golbraikh2014} scores a binary dataset by a class-weighted share of nearest-neighbor pairs that share an activity class, producing a single value for how strongly the similarity assumption holds on that particular combination of representation and data. It is not clear whether a low value is due to the data being intrinsically hard to model or to the representation not capturing adequate features for the task. Other work in this area relates to activity cliffs. \citet{Sheridan2020} compared experimental error, activity distribution and activity-cliff metrics as predictors of how well a dataset can be modeled, and found the cliff metrics the strongest of the three. However, they found that these metrics cannot separate real cliffs from those produced by uncertain activity measurements. \citet{MedinaFranco2013} point out that some of these activity cliffs are artifacts of the chosen representation, similarity threshold, or noise in the data. Activity cliffs are distinct from noise, as they carry some of the most valuable information about the interaction between structure and activity. None of these measures ask specifically about whether the specific features within these representations matter for the property at hand. 

% TODO(cite): Praski2025 says "nearly all"; CLAMP was significantly better.
We first consider how much the representation impacts similarity measures. \citet{Riniker2013} benchmarked 12 fingerprints and two simple baseline fingerprints for similarity-based virtual screening on 88 targets from three public collections, and found that the screening performance of the 12 fingerprints was highly correlated. Only the two baselines differed significantly from the rest. Nearly all of 25 pretrained neural embeddings were found to have a negligible improvement over ECFP across 25 datasets \citep{Praski2025}. However, when looking into the differences between the structure of fingerprints, \citet{Huber2026} found that fingerprints which store raw counts are more effective at defining similarity than folded fingerprints when compared against a graph-based structural reference. Folding introduces bit collisions, which disproportionately distort similarity for molecules with many set bits \citep{Huber2026}. ACs follow the same pattern: across 15 configurations of representation and similarity measures on 3 datasets, the representations disagreed about which pairs form cliffs \citep{DabrowskiTumanski2026}, supporting the point \citet{MedinaFranco2013} make about ACs being artifacts of their representation. 

Any method that incorporates similarity measures, such as AC detection, or cleaning a training set by comparing each sample's label with its nearest neighbors' labels, whether by removing the samples that disagree \citep{Wilson1972} or by smoothing their labels, inherits this choice, as do the noise estimation and mitigation methods of this thesis. \citet{Coupry2022} learn a molecular similarity from graph embeddings, but independently of any endpoint. \citet{Zheng2000} did choose the similarity for the task. They predict each molecule's activity as the average over its $k$ most similar molecules by Euclidean distance over descriptors, and choose the descriptors by simulated annealing to maximize the leave-one-out $q^2$. \citet{Itskowitz2005} also optimize the number of neighbors and their weighting. Both select features for a nearest-neighbor model of one endpoint, whereas this thesis selects features across several representations.

The purpose of this chapter is to establish the problem that Chapter~\ref{chap:method} sets out to solve. Section~\ref{sec:flaws:representations} asks, for a given dataset, how much the features of standard representations vary and how many molecules map to identical feature vectors. Section~\ref{sec:flaws:similarity} asks what happens when these features are used to quantify similarity, and Section~\ref{sec:flaws:consequences} follows the consequences for methods built on these similarity measures, including a small sweep of published noise-handling methods (Section~\ref{sec:lnl_motivation}).

