These constant counts are sometimes filtered out by the distance. Tanimoto similarity (Equation~\ref{eq:tanimoto}; the distance is $1-T$) ignores bits off in both molecules, but a bit on in every molecule raises every molecule's measured similarity. It does not use the 105 constant ECFP4 bits on hERG $K_i$. Constant features add zero to a Euclidean or Manhattan distance; however, near-constant features are amplified. That's great behavior if those features define an AC. If not, they are contributing disproportionately to the measure of similarity.

\subsubsection{Molecules with identical feature vectors}\label{sec:flaws:ties}

A standard fingerprint- or descriptor-based representation can produce the same feature vector for two separate molecules. In turn, models using this representation will produce a single prediction for the molecules with identical features. Identical feature vectors do not always mean that the representation has merged two different molecules, since a dataset pooled from several sources can hold two measurements of the same molecule.

Many fingerprints are computed without stereochemistry \citep{orsi2024map4c}. In this research, ECFP4 is built with stereochemistry off, and the 2D descriptor sets (PDV, Mordred) also ignore it, so almost every molecule they produce identical representations for is a stereoisomer of another (Table~\ref{tab:flaws:ties}). Any representation that reads from these subsequently merges stereoisomers. Additionally, methods using the Weisfeiler--Lehman relabeling scheme are bounded by the 1-Weisfeiler--Lehman test (Chapter~\ref{chap:background}), so some non-isomorphic graphs also receive the same vector. This is seen in the graph-kernel features which tie 105 of 1,412 hERG $K_i$ molecules, 56 of which differ only in stereochemistry and 49 in more than that. 

Whether or not a learned embedding is able to differentiate stereoisomers is dependent on its input as well as its individual implementation. SMI-TED, a pretrained SMILES encoder, ties 56 hERG $K_i$, 633 LogD and 177 Caco-2 molecules, all stereoisomers. A tokenizer can drop stereochemistry prior to training. The tokenizer of the ChemBERTa-77M-MTR checkpoint (ChemBERTa-2 in Additional file~\ref{app:add:constant_features}, called ChemBERTa in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}) gives 72 of the 1,415 hERG $K_i$ molecules of Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness} the same token sequence as another molecule. Since stereoisomers can differ in measured activity \citep{Nguyen2006}, these representations lack potentially useful chemical information. 

% TODO: manual review
Two molecules are tied when a representation gives them exactly the same feature vector. Table~\ref{tab:flaws:ties} counts ties on hERG $K_i$, LogD and Caco-2 for each dataset's base representations and for its augmentation features, the small feature sets added to the hybrid after it is built (Section~\ref{sec:method:augmentation}). No tie came from one compound listed twice (checked by InChIKey, which differs between stereoisomers) or from a row of zeros.

Apart from the MACCS keys, most tied molecules are stereoisomers. Atom pairs, PDV, Mordred and SMI-TED tie only stereoisomers, and ECFP4, Avalon and topological torsions add a few molecules that differ in more than their stereochemistry. On hERG $K_i$, ECFP4, atom pairs, PDV, Mordred and SMI-TED tie the same 56 molecules, so joining any of these five representations leaves all 56 tied. The WL graph kernel also ties 49 hERG $K_i$ and 26 Caco-2 molecules that are not stereoisomers, and the MACCS keys tie the most molecules on every dataset, mostly not stereoisomers. The 3D augmentation features and the learned embeddings ChemBERT, GROVER, MHG-GNN, MoLFormer and GraphMVP tie none, and joining all of a dataset's representations leaves no molecule tied.
% TODO: the ChemBERT count comes from the cached chembert.npy (256 features); whether that cache matches the current featurizer code is unverified.

A model has to give every molecule in a tied group the same prediction. The best single prediction for a group is its mean label, so ties set an error that no model can go below,
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{RMSE}_{\min} = \sqrt{\frac{1}{n}\sum_{G}\sum_{i \in G}\left(y_i - \bar{y}_G\right)^2},
\end{equation}
where $n$ is the number of molecules, $G$ runs over the tied groups and $\bar{y}_G$ is the mean label of group $G$. The matching best $R^2$ is $1 - \mathrm{RMSE}_{\min}^2 / \mathrm{Var}(y)$. On hERG $K_i$ this lowest error is at most $0.134$ p$K_i$ (MACCS keys), small next to the label standard deviation of $0.915$ p$K_i$.

Single groups can still disagree by a lot. Independent p$K_i$ measurements of the same compound against the same target differ by $0.44$ log units on average, with a standard deviation of $0.54$ \citep{Kramer2012}, so a gap larger than $0.54$ inside a tied group is larger than typical measurement noise. On hERG $K_i$, 6 of the 26 ECFP4 groups and 38 of the 153 MACCS groups differ by more than $0.54$. The widest ECFP4 group is a pair of stereoisomers $1.14$ p$K_i$ apart (Figure~\ref{fig:flaws:stereo_pair}). An ECFP4 model can at best predict their mean, $5.59$ p$K_i$, which leaves each molecule $0.57$ p$K_i$ from its measured value.

