The four measurements below use the six small-molecule regression datasets of Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps} (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}) and HLM clearance. Binary representations were compared by Tanimoto distance, and all others by Manhattan distance on standardized features (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:methods}), with constant features removed.

\subsubsection{Activity cliffs}
Chapter~\ref{chap:background} gives the MoleculeACE definition of an activity cliff, which combines three similarity measures. Here we use one. Two molecules are similar if their ECFP4 Tanimoto similarity is at least 0.55 \citep{Hu2015}, and a molecule is a cliff if a similar molecule has a label that differs from its own by more than a set gap. We chose the gap on each dataset so that 15\% of the molecules are cliffs, so the gap follows the spread of the labels (Table~\ref{tab:flaws:distance_summary}). On hERG $K_i$ the gap is 1.76 p$K_i$, and 211 of the 1,412 molecules are cliffs. On QM9 only 1,093 pairs of the 9,978 molecules are similar, and the gap is 0.05~eV, 0.06 times the median absolute deviation of the labels. On the three Biogen datasets (RLM clearance, MDR1--MDCK efflux and HLM clearance) it is 0.20 to 0.34 log units, 0.50 to 0.60 times the median absolute deviation.

The count of cliffs depends on the representation. We kept each dataset's gap and replaced ECFP4 with each other base representation, calling similar the same number of closest pairs as ECFP4 does. On hERG $K_i$ the number of cliff molecules then ranges from 138 (atom pairs) to 267 (WL graph kernel). Of the pairs that ECFP4 calls similar, only 57\% to 74\% are also among another representation's closest pairs, and on QM9 only 1\% to 24\% (Additional file~\ref{app:add:flaws}).

\subsubsection{Distance and label difference}
We asked whether distance tracks label difference in two ways. Over all pairs of molecules, we took the Spearman correlation between the distance of a pair and the absolute difference of its labels, a one-number summary of the neighborhood-behavior plot of \citet{Patterson1996}; datasets above 2,000 molecules were sampled down to 2,000. Over each molecule's closest pairs only, we predicted each label as the mean label of the molecule's five nearest neighbors, taken from other scaffold groups, and took the Spearman correlation between predicted and measured labels.

Across the 70 combinations of dataset and representation, the all-pairs correlation ranged from $-0.04$ to 0.23 and was below 0.10 in 44 of them. The five-neighbor prediction reached 0.29 to 0.89, higher than the all-pairs correlation in all 70 (Table~\ref{tab:flaws:distance_summary}; every representation is in Additional file~\ref{app:add:flaws}). On hERG $K_i$ with ECFP4, the all-pairs correlation is 0.06, while the five-neighbor prediction reaches 0.72, with an RMSE of 0.63 p$K_i$ against a label standard deviation of 0.92 p$K_i$.

Figure~\ref{fig:flaws:herg_pairs} shows why the two differ. Of the 996,166 hERG $K_i$ pairs, 93\% have an ECFP4 Tanimoto similarity below 0.20, and only 1.1\% reach 0.55. Over all pairs the median label difference is 0.81 p$K_i$, but above a similarity of about 0.4 it is 0.20 to 0.30 p$K_i$, although 8.3\% of the 774 pairs at 0.8 or above still differ by more than 1 p$K_i$. Mordred shows the same pattern: the closest 5\% of pairs have a median difference of 0.44 p$K_i$, and every other 5\% has 0.78 to 1.11 p$K_i$. The useful information sits in the few closest pairs. The all-pairs correlation is set by the other 95\%, whereas the five-neighbor prediction uses only the closest pairs.

\subsubsection{Agreement on neighbors}
We compared the five nearest neighbors of each molecule, from other scaffold groups, under ECFP4, SMI-TED and Mordred (PDV on Caco-2, where Mordred is not a base representation; on HLM clearance ECFP4 is not a base representation and its stored features were used). On hERG $K_i$ any two of the three share on average 0.47 to 0.52 of each molecule's five neighbors, and on the three Biogen datasets and QM9 only 0.13 to 0.21 (Table~\ref{tab:flaws:neighbor_overlap}).

\subsubsection{Agreement with shared structure}
For 500 random pairs per dataset we found the maximum common substructure (MCS) with RDKit \citep{rdkit}, allowing 2 seconds per pair and leaving out pairs that ran out of time. The MCS distance is $1 - n/(a+b-n)$, where $n$ is the number of atoms in the common substructure and $a$ and $b$ are the numbers of heavy atoms in the two molecules. Its Spearman correlation with ECFP4 Tanimoto distance ranged from 0.20 (hERG $K_i$ and HLM clearance) to 0.51 (Caco-2 and LogD).

Every method in Section~\ref{sec:flaws:consequences} takes a similarity as input and inherits the dilution described here.

