We've identified some issues within the representations and similarities computed over them. While this calls into question the predictive limits of QSAR and other structure-based modeling, it also calls into question downstream applications of molecular similarity. Nearest-neighbor methods, where a molecule's neighbors, as measured by similarity, are the inputs to several other processes, are by definition sensitive to the quality of similarity measurements. They are used within other processes like predicting a molecule's activity using its neighbors' labels, detecting outliers, either ACs or noise, defining an applicability domain by distance to the training set \citep{Tropsha2010}, grouping molecules into clusters for data splits \citep{Butina1999}, identifying activity cliffs, and, in federated learning, weighting each site's vote on a molecule by that molecule's distance to the site's own data \citep{Hanser2025}. If this measure of similarity, or equivalently distance, is flawed, then all of these downstream applications will feel the effects of it.

