Predicting each label from its five nearest neighbors gave a higher Spearman correlation in all 70, so the useful information sits in a few close pairs rather than in the distance as a whole.

% TODO: manual review
We found that methods built on a distance did worst under label noise. In the small sweep of published noise-handling methods, the configurations with the lowest retention all used a distance (Section~\ref{sec:lnl_motivation}), and judging a label by its neighbors' labels usually made the model worse (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:neighbors}).

A representation that avoids these flaws would need five properties (Table~\ref{tab:flaws:requirements}). Its features would be chosen for the task, using only each fold's training labels. It would be compact, dropping features that do not vary or do not matter, with the number kept searched rather than fixed. It would draw on several representations, since each misses information the others capture; plain concatenation does this but is not compact (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}, ``Combining representations''). It would work both as a model input and as a space for similarity. Finally, it would be traceable: each chosen feature leads back to its representation and can be read chemically wherever the base feature can.

Feature selection cannot fix every flaw. Molecules identical in every base representation stay identical in the hybrid, and a selected hashed bit still merges every substructure folded into it. Selection also needs enough labeled molecules to estimate which features matter.

