Not every molecular representation is equal. In fact, they hold very different types of information. RDKit2D descriptors matched or outperformed features derived from embedded GNNs and pretrained language models on many datasets across 62,820 models on a variety of molecular property prediction benchmarks \citep{Deng2023}. Descriptor-based models held 73\% of top-three positions on 11 benchmarks \citep{Jiang2021}. In 1,310 ChEMBL assays, feedforward networks on ECFP fingerprints outperformed graph convolutions and recurrent networks trained on SMILES \citep{Mayr2018}. We know that the choice of representation matters, as is discussed in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}, yet the combinations of representation and model are hard to explore by hand \citep{Lanini2023PREFER}. Beyond just molecules, general protein language models predict antibody expression better than antibody-specific ones, while the antibody-specific models are better on binding \citep{Kenlay2024}, and no pathology foundation model is best on every clinical task \citep{ElNahhas2025}. We turn our attention to combining features from these different representations. 

Most research in this field relies on a single representation, and few combine features from different representations beyond concatenation. One of the reasons just pooling features from different representations together doesn't work is dimensionality. If you add a standard ECFP4 ($d=2,048$) with Mordred descriptors ($d=1,613$) and an MHG-GNN embedding ($d=1,024$), you end up with 4,685 features, which is far too many considering many datasets from experimental assays contain only a few hundred labeled samples. With such ratios, more features mean weaker predictions \citep{Hughes1968, Bellman1961}. Therefore, we consider ways of combining representations. 

% TODO: I'm pretty sure I'm doing late fusion but calling it ensembling, make sure its appropriately named below
The most straightforward method of combining representations is concatenation. This however degrades with non-homogeneous feature sets and obscures which representation is responsible for the score \citep{Stahlschmidt2022}. \citet{Deng2023} found that appending RDKit2D descriptors to GROVER lowered RMSE on 9 of 24 datasets tested. Late fusion trains one model per source and aggregates the predictions \citep{Steyaert2023}. MOGONET is of this kind, combining per-source predictions in label space \citep{Wang2021}. While this is effective, it loses interactions between the source representations, an issue which is addressed by intermediate fusion, for example cross-attention between sources \citep{Beaude2025}. Multiple kernel learning does this with kernels rather than whole models \citep{Gonen2011, Cichonska2018}, and produces no interpretable feature sets. \citet{Laufktter2019} found that combining bioactivity-based fingerprints with structure-based ones like ECFP4 improved both predictive performance and scaffold-hopping. Similarly, \citet{Turkina2025} combined features from six non-hashed fingerprints with feature importance scores and showed that the additional features consistently improved predictive accuracy over the original fingerprints across a range of models. Most of this work involves limited combinations, adding together standard fingerprints and linear representations \citep{Xie2020, Laufktter2019}. Although the features derived from embeddings are not as conceptually straightforward, they contain another axis of information. 

When you want to filter a feature set for the features most important to the prediction task, you can use a dimensionality reduction technique or feature selection methodology. Principal component analysis (PCA) uses linear combinations of the original features, ordered by the variance they explain \citep{Jolliffe2016}. Their combined features are dependent on the original feature units unless each feature is z-scored first which matters when data from different modalities like fingerprint bits, descriptors and embeddings are joined \citep{Jolliffe2016}. PCA never sees the labels, and components with small variance can still be important in prediction \citep{Jolliffe1982}. Partial least squares (PLS), the supervised counterpart in chemometrics, builds components that covary with the property \citep{Wold2001}. Neither gives components that can be traced to one substructure, descriptor or representation, making them difficult to interpret.

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: mRMR is mutual information with the label minus redundancy
Feature selection keeps a subset of the original features, unlike PCA or PLS which builds new ones. Of its three families \citep{Guyon2003}, filter methods score features against the labels before any model is fitted, as minimum-redundancy maximum-relevance selection (mRMR) ranks features by their mutual information with the label minus their redundancy with already-chosen features \citep{Peng2005}. Wrapper methods score subsets of features by fitting the model \citep{Kohavi1997}. Embedded methods select features while the model is fitted, as the lasso does \citep{Tibshirani1996}. Recursive feature elimination repeatedly fits a model and drops the lowest-ranked features \citep{Guyon2002}.

When selecting features, the question is not only what features are most important, but also, what features does the model prefer? One of the ways to answer this is to consider how many features relative to the size of a dataset a given model works best with. Distance-based models such as $k$-nearest neighbors (kNN) and SVM use every feature in every distance computation (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}), whereas tree ensembles choose one feature per split and can essentially ignore irrelevant ones. \citet{sns}, who created a substructure-based fingerprint called Sort \& Slice, suggest that using labels to select features may result in overfitting. Table~\ref{tab:hybrid_alternatives} compares these alternatives with the adaptive hybrid methodology developed in this thesis. 

