\subsection{The adaptive hybrid representation}

The task of selecting the best features from a set of representations and combining them is not simple. How do you measure what features are ``best''? Are these features ``best'' relative to the representation they came from or to the whole pool of features? Should you select more features from representations that seem to have more signal to begin with, or take a set number of features from every representation? How many base representations should you select features from? How many features should you select? When scoring potential combinations of features, what type of evaluator should you use?

In a dream world, there would be a single answer to each one of these questions that applies to every dataset. Unfortunately we live in reality, and as I discovered, the answer to every single one of these questions is ``depends on the data.'' The task of selecting the best features then became a search space optimization challenge. The question actually became for a given prediction task, defined by the dataset, prediction target, and model, what is the best methodology for selecting features? The hybrid search does not output just a list of features, rather it outputs an ideal search configuration to find features for your data. It tells you how to score features (feature importance score), what rules you should use to select them (allocation method), how to score potential hybrid feature combinations (internal evaluator). In this research, I run this hybrid search multiple times across different maximum number of features (budgets), number of input representations, and models (downstream evaluators) on each dataset. While it is not formal ``training'' in the ML sense, the hybrid search is essentially a feature selection method that is fit on the training data. The representations used in this thesis are listed in Tables~\ref{tab:reps_smallmol}, \ref{tab:reps_reactions} and~\ref{tab:reps_antibodies}. % TODO: manual review

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: "fixed number of" removed; the hybrid can have fewer features than its budget
Formally, an adaptive hybrid representation is a set of features, capped at a fixed budget, selected from several base representations of the same molecules. The output of a hybrid search is itself a feature selection method that when run on the given dataset, produces the hybrid representation. This search is run on a single dataset and downstream model, and the resulting hybrid differs by model, dataset, and even split. Its intended use case is when there are multiple base representations that describe different aspects of a molecule, and you want to combine these different features for a single training task. The hybrid ranks features by a fitted model's importance scores, as embedded methods do, and keeps a fixed number of the top-ranked ones, as a filter does. Classical methods treat all features as one flat list. The nearest classical relative, the group lasso, keeps or drops predefined groups of variables whole \citep{Yuan2006}, whereas the hybrid splits the budget among representations (Section~\ref{sec:method:allocation}). Because only original features are kept, each traces back to one representation, which makes blueprints possible.

\subsection{Blueprints and their chemical interpretation}

As explained above, the adaptive hybrid representation relies on a search. A completed search is saved as a blueprint, which records the settings needed to select the features that make the hybrid representation. Because each selected feature traces back to its original base representation and relative importance rank, the search can tell you about your own prediction task. The best configuration is saved for each downstream model and budget, giving one blueprint per pair, allowing us to compare blueprints and see if different models prefer different features. 

Depending on the origin of the feature, some are chemically interpretable. By definition, the PDV contains values that relate back to chemical properties like molecular weight or logP. An ECFP4 bit can be shown as the atom environment that sets it, and a MACCS key stands for a known substructure. Beyond small molecules, the readable unit of an antibody is a sequence position and its residue. This feature-level explainability fails when features are derived from learned embeddings. That will only tell you if the learned embedding itself is useful as a representation, but nothing about the selected feature. 

While a set of thousands of features may seem overwhelming and irrelevant, identifying substructures or descriptors that recur among the selected features can point to parts of the molecule that may be involved in binding or activity. If the selected features make sense chemically for that endpoint, it's a good sign that the selection mechanism is finding real signal instead of fitting noise. Conversely, if the hybrid search is done over a range of budgets or models, and the blueprints are showing a vastly different set of features, that may be an indication that the features at hand are struggling to provide information about the prediction task. A predictor that scores proposed molecules in generative design \citep{Olivecrona2017} could also take a hybrid as input, which we leave to future work.

\subsection{Applications beyond small molecules}

The hybrid search is a generalized search that takes in multiple feature matrices from the same samples and a supervised prediction task, and outputs a feature selection method that produces a filtered set of features from those original vectors. The default search objective scores prediction only, meaning the methodology applies to tabular tasks or cases where you have different measurements of the same inputs. Although this was developed with small molecules and QSAR modeling in mind, it is by no means limited to that use case. While there are some functionalities in this research that are tailored to chemical tasks, such as blueprints mapping features back to their chemical interpretations and the use of nearest-neighbor objectives (Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}) to aid in the computation of molecular similarity, the core functionality of the hybrid process is domain-independent.

