To run a hybrid search, we first measure feature importance, select how many features to take from each contributing base representation, and choose how to score a candidate feature set. And for each of those steps, there's no one right way to do it. Different prediction tasks prefer different methods of scoring feature importance or candidate features, different sets of input representations, and so forth. The search tries to find the ideal settings for the hybrid feature selection process, tailored to a given dataset and downstream model. All combinations of settings values are explored, as the settings interact. The best budget depends on the downstream model, as shown below for nanobody thermostability. This search grid also tests the chapter's claim that no single setting suits every dataset.

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: table description updated; that table no longer has speed tiers
Six settings in total are searched, namely the downstream model, the budget, the representation count, the importance method, the allocation method and the internal evaluator. Table~\ref{tab:search_tiers} gives the values each setting can take, and Table~\ref{tab:decided_grid} the smaller set searched on each dataset in this chapter (Section~\ref{sec:method:decided_grid}). The importance methods tested are LightGBM gain, TreeSHAP or DeepLIFT (Section~\ref{sec:method:importance}). Feature-level importance depends on the model as well as on the data \citep{Dong2020}, and as such we observe that within the search, a different ranking of features produces a different hybrid output.

The downstream model is trained on the hybrid representation. This model can be a supervised model of any form, classification or regression. We used random forest, XGBoost, LightGBM, SVM, a deep neural network (DNN) and a Gaussian process. Each dataset in this chapter uses two of them (Section~\ref{sec:method:models}). 

The budget is the maximum number of features in the hybrid, taken from eight values (10, 25, 50, 100, 250, 500, 1,000 and 2,000 features). When the budget is too small, informative features can be left out, yet when it is too large it will pick up on too many uninformative ones, especially ones that give small datasets room to overfit.

We select a set of up to 10 base representations per dataset, chosen prior to the search (Table~\ref{tab:dataset_reps}). The selections were made based on each representation's relative performance on the prediction task. We took into account what type of information each representation contained, and tried to span a wide set across the selected 10. For example, while there are many high-performing graph-based embeddings in QSAR modeling, for the purposes of testing a feature selection process which takes features from different sources of information, it is better to have a mix of graph-based embeddings, as well as other types of molecular representations like substructure fingerprints, property vectors, sequential embeddings, etc.  The representation count (2, 3, 5 or all) sets how many may contribute features, keeping those with the highest standalone scores on each fold. The number of representation counts to test is searched in the sweep as datasets differ in how many representations have features worth selecting, and pooled feature selection over all representations has no such setting. While we tested various methods of pooling the features, we found computing feature importance scores and concatenation techniques on a global pooled feature set was far less effective than when the feature scoring is kept on a per-representation basis. Since the features contain different types of information at different scales, pooling the features too early makes comparisons within the pool challenging. We found that it's better to determine which features are important to each representation first, and go from there.

Every allocation method is paired with every count. In preliminary runs, some datasets performed best with 2 or 3 representations and others with all 10.
