\subsubsection{Search configuration and internal evaluation}

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: twelve types are what the code supports; the thesis uses six
The most useful features for an RF may not be the most useful for an SVM, and vice versa. The relative importance of a feature is dependent on the model \citep{Dong2020}. Within the search grid, we use an internal evaluator to fit on one candidate feature set at a time. This process is separate from the final downstream model evaluation. In a standard search, four internal evaluators are used: RF, DNN, LightGBM, and the matched evaluator. When the internal evaluator is set to \texttt{match}, the search scores each candidate hybrid on the same model type as the downstream model, for each of the twelve model types the code supports. So internal evaluators outside the standard sweep may be tested. For a downstream model outside of the pre-defined set, if no matched evaluator exists in the code, the search uses the other internal evaluators that were requested. Each internal evaluator is fitted on the internal training fold and scores on the internal validation fold within that CV iteration using the dataset's specified evaluation metric. 

% TODO: manual review
A random forest, LightGBM or DNN downstream model is searched with three internal evaluators, because its matched evaluator is already one of the other three. An XGBoost, SVM or Gaussian process downstream model is searched with four, because the matched evaluator adds one of its own family. Only the DNN evaluator shares its downstream model's architecture and training settings. Even it draws its 20\% early-stopping samples at random, whereas the downstream DNN splits them by the outer split's groups. Additional file~\ref{app:add:model_settings} gives every evaluator setting.

The internal evaluator's scores determine how the budget is split and which representations are kept. The internal evaluator ranks candidate feature sets, it explicitly does not rank features within a representation. This process of selecting a feature set by fitting a model on it, and keeping the set that scores the best is known as the wrapper method \citep{Kohavi1997}. This is distinct from a filter method which scores features without fitting a model. 

