\subsection{Evaluation protocol}\label{sec:method:evaluation}

% TODO: manual review
Every experiment splits each dataset into outer folds as set out in Section~\ref{sec:method:datasets}. The search sees only the training samples of each outer fold, and the selected features are rebuilt on them and applied unchanged to the held-out samples. Only three experiments limit each held-out feature to the range it takes on the training samples: the comparison with the best single representation, the easy and hard halves, and the comparison of many and few representations.

% TODO: manual review
Because of time and computational constraints, not every experiment in Chapters~\ref{chap:method} to~\ref{chap:noise} ran on every fold or every dataset. Some experiments here ran on the first three outer folds, most noise experiments in Chapter~\ref{chap:noise} ran on the first outer fold only, and some runs had not finished when this thesis was written. We report the results that exist, give the number of folds beside each one, and name the datasets where a result is missing. We do not repeat this caveat at every result.

% TODO: manual review
The hybrid is compared with the best single base representation at its full number of features, which is what a user would otherwise run. This comparator is chosen per downstream model and per fold from the search's scores on the outer training samples, and it can differ between folds. It is then refit on those samples and scored on the same held-out samples as the hybrid. The hybrid is held to its budget, and the comparator is consequently often many times wider. Both widths are recorded for every fold, since a difference between the two scores is partly a difference in width. The main quantity per fold is the hybrid's score minus that of the best single representation, signed so that a positive value means the hybrid scored better (for RMSE the sign is flipped).

