\subsection{Representations by domain}\label{sec:method:reps}
% TODO: manual review

Any representation that gives one fixed-length vector per sample can serve as a base representation (Section~\ref{sec:method:build}). The first version of the method used three molecular representations: PDV, ECFP4 and MHG-GNN. These three are now one small-molecule example among many. Every base representation is frozen: it is computed once from the raw input (a SMILES string, a sequence or a reaction) and is never trained on the labels.

To choose them, every candidate representation was first scored on its own with a gradient-boosted tree model \citep{ke2017lightgbm} under the dataset's grouped folds. Candidates were then compared by the Spearman correlation between their out-of-fold predictions, since two representations whose predictions correlate highly carry much the same signal. At most ten were then chosen by hand, looking at each candidate's own score next to these correlations and favoring representations that encode the input in different ways. Binding free energy has eight. % TODO: manual review

% TODO: manual review
Which representation is best depends on the downstream model as well as the dataset. The best one is the same under both of a dataset's downstream models on three of the seven small-molecule datasets and five of the twelve antibody datasets (Table~\ref{tab:datasets}). On hERG $K_i$, for example, MHG-GNN is best with the SVM and GROVER with the DNN. Augmentation features do not count toward the ten, and no search in this chapter uses them (Section~\ref{sec:method:augmentation}).

If an input cannot be parsed or embedded, it gets an all-zero row, so that every representation of a dataset keeps the same rows. In computed descriptors, a missing or infinite value is set to 0, and values are stored unscaled. A pretrained model gives one vector per input, the mean of its last-layer token vectors with padding excluded, unless the tables say otherwise. Scaling and filling of missing values happen inside each fold, on the training rows only, as described in the construction of the hybrid.

\subsubsection{Small molecules}
The small-molecule representations (Table~\ref{tab:reps_smallmol}) are the fingerprint, descriptor, graph and language-model families described in Chapter~\ref{chap:background}. The only additions are four sets of 3D shape and pharmacophore features, used as augmentation only. All four are computed from a single conformer per molecule, embedded with a fixed seed and optimized with the Merck molecular force field. A single conformer may misdescribe flexible molecules.

\subsubsection{Chemical reactions}
Reaction datasets (Table~\ref{tab:reps_reactions}) use three kinds of representation. The condensed graph of reaction (CGR) merges reactants and products into one graph in which the bonds that change are marked \citep{Varnek2005, Hoonakker2011}. Here it is hashed into a circular fingerprint after atom mapping \citep{Schwaller2021rxnmapper}. Reaction language models read the reaction SMILES directly \citep{Schwaller2021rxnfp, Sagawa2023ReactionT5}. Any molecular representation can also be applied to the reactants, to the product, or to their difference. A one-hot encoding of which aryl halide, ligand, base and additive was used carries no chemistry. It is kept because on plate data such identity features can predict yield about as well as chemical descriptors \citep{Chuang2018}.

% TODO: manual review -- fixed: CDR spelled out at first use
\subsubsection{Antibodies}
Antibody representations (Table~\ref{tab:reps_antibodies}) start from the heavy- and light-chain amino-acid sequences. Besides one-hot and BLOSUM62 encodings \citep{Henikoff1992}, most are frozen language models, either trained on all proteins or on antibody repertoires only. Most models embed each chain separately and join the two vectors, so their width doubles on datasets with both chains. Several representations read one model in different ways, for instance averaging over earlier layers or over the complementarity-determining region (CDR) residues only, with CDRs found by IMGT numbering \citep{Lefranc2003} using ANARCI \citep{Dunbar2016ANARCI}. Datasets of point variants of one parent antibody also use features that describe each mutation.

