\subsubsection{Best single representation and PCA}
% TODO: manual review
As a second reference beside the best single representation, every base representation is joined, standardized on the training samples and reduced by PCA \citep{Jolliffe2016} to each budget of the dataset's grid. The downstream model is fitted on the principal components as they are. On each fold, the hybrid is compared with PCA at the hybrid's own budget; in this way, neither side's width is chosen on the held-out samples. PCA cannot have more components than training samples, and where the budget is larger the two widths differ. % TODO: manual review

\subsubsection{Ensembles}
% TODO: manual review
The ensemble fits one model per representation and pools their predictions (late fusion), whereas the hybrid fits one model on one fused feature vector (early fusion) \citep{Stahlschmidt2022}. Every choice for the ensemble is made on an inner 3-fold group split of the outer training samples. The first ensemble uses the same downstream model on every representation, and the second uses each representation's own better model. Each picks its members, the top 3 representations or all of them, and one of seven ways to pool predictions, from the mean and median to trimmed, outlier-removed and score-weighted means.

\subsubsection{Many or few representations}
% TODO: manual review
On each outer fold, the base representations are ranked by their summed feature importance on the training samples. The top 1, 2 or 3 of them supply the $B$ features with the highest importance among their own features. These are compared with the search's own hybrid at $B$ and with an even split of $B$ over every base representation, each using the importance method that the fold's best hybrid used. The search's hybrid differs from the hand-built hybrids in two ways at once. It ranks representations by their standalone scores rather than by summed importance, and it stops at the last feature with importance above zero.

\subsubsection{Easy and hard halves}
% TODO: manual review
Datasets above 2{,}000 samples are first reduced to about 2{,}000 by drawing whole scaffold families with a fixed seed. Scaffolds at or above the 80th percentile of group size form the easy half, and the rest form the hard half. Each half gets its own search on each outer fold. The hybrid of a search on all the subset's training samples is also rebuilt and scored on each half. Three other splits were run as well (by heavy-atom count, label spread within a scaffold and feature spread within a scaffold), which are not reported here.

\subsubsection{Overfitting with the budget}
% TODO: manual review
The budgets are the standard list (Table~\ref{tab:search_tiers}) plus 4{,}000 and 8{,}000 features. They also include 0.5, 0.75, 1, 1.25, 1.5 and 2 times the smallest outer training set, which places one budget where the number of features equals the number of samples. At every budget, the winning configuration is rebuilt on the outer training samples and scored on the held-out fold. The selection bias at a budget is the search's own score minus the held-out score; a positive value means the search promised more than the held-out fold gave. We report how the bias changes with the budget, and the budget the search picks on each fold from its training samples alone. We do not compare hybrids wider than the training set against narrower ones by their best held-out budgets, because that choice reads the held-out labels on both sides.

\subsubsection{One fold fewer}
% TODO: manual review
A second check repeats each outer fold's search with one more fold removed from its training samples, for each of the other four folds. The held-out fold is the same and the two searches differ by one fold of training samples. Each search, however, picks its own budget, so the two hybrids can differ in width as well.

\subsubsection{Feature reduction at the same width}
% TODO: manual review
On regression datasets, every method receives $B$ features from all base representations joined, fitted on the fold's training samples. The methods are the two best hybrids at budget $B$ from the fold's search, mRMR \citep{Peng2005}, recursive feature elimination \citep{Guyon2002} and $B$ random features. mRMR ranks features by mutual information and stops at 200 features for run time. It consequently has no result on any small-molecule dataset, where every budget is 250 features or more. Recursive elimination fits a 50-tree random forest of depth 5 and removes 10\% of the features at each step. Each of the dataset's two downstream models scores every method, and only the features differ.

\subsubsection{Internal evaluator}
% TODO: manual review
One search per outer fold is run for each evaluator setting. The matched evaluator belongs to the downstream model's family, while a near-family evaluator pairs tree models with each other, the SVM with a Gaussian process, and the DNN with a narrower DNN. A far-family evaluator pairs tree models with an SVM, the SVM with LightGBM, and the DNN with $k$-nearest neighbors. A fourth setting scores the DNN with a wider DNN, and a fifth searches the dataset's own internal evaluators (Table~\ref{tab:decided_grid}). Each setting's search picks its own budget, and the number of features can thus differ between settings. The SVM's near-family evaluator ran before the Gaussian-process correction of 8 October 2026 on every dataset.

\subsubsection{Importance during training}
% TODO: manual review
Importance is computed on the training samples by the method the search picked. Sample weighting is not run for the Gaussian process, which takes no per-sample weights, nor on multiclass tasks. The five conditions are:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
    \item No weighting: the features as selected.
    \item Feature weighting: standardized features multiplied by the square root of their importance, with the weights normalized to average 1.
    \item Sample weighting: samples weighted by how well their label agrees with their 10 nearest neighbors in the rescaled space.
    \item Combined weighting: feature and sample weighting together.
    \item Above-median selection: only the features strictly above the median importance.
\end{enumerate}
The weight of sample $i$ is $1 - |y_i - \bar y_{N(i)}| / (\max y - \min y)$, clipped to lie between 0.1 and 1, where $\bar y_{N(i)}$ is the mean label of its 10 nearest neighbors by Manhattan distance. Tree splits depend only on the order of values \citep{Hastie2009}, so for tree models feature weighting differs from no weighting only in that zero-importance features drop out. The rescaling does change which samples are nearest neighbors, and so changes sample weighting. Sample weighting down-weights rather than removes a suspect label, unlike neighbor-based filtering (Section~\ref{sec:flaws:neighbors}). We found no QSAR study that does this, and learned per-sample weights for noisy labels come from general machine learning \citep{shu2019metaweightnet}.

\subsubsection{Fewer features or a tuned model}
% TODO: manual review
For the SVM and the random forest, the best hybrid at a small budget is fitted at the model's default setting. It is compared with a hybrid rebuilt at the largest width we allow: the smallest of the pool width, twice the number of training samples and 2{,}048 features. This wider hybrid is fitted at the setting that scores best on the other folds (the SVM's $C$ or the forest's maximum depth). Datasets whose two downstream models include neither family are not run.

\subsubsection{Adding a new embedding}
% TODO: manual review
Each embedding of the pool is taken in turn as the new one, and the search is run with it and without it. As a control, the embedding's selected features in the hybrid are replaced by the same number of Gaussian random features, with no new search. On LogD and hERG blocking the experiment ran on 2{,}000 molecules, drawn as whole scaffold families.

\subsubsection{Calibration}
% TODO: manual review
Split-conformal 90\% prediction intervals are built on the training samples and scored on the held-out samples by the fraction of held-out labels they cover and by their mean width.

\subsubsection{Stability and blueprints}
% TODO: manual review
Selection on fold $m$, for $m = 1, \dots, F$, gives a binary vector $\mathbf{z}_m \in \{0,1\}^d$. Here $d$ is the number of features in all base representations joined, not the size of the union of the selected features. We measure the stability of the selection with the index of \citet{Nogueira2018},
\[
\hat\Phi = 1 - \frac{\frac1d\sum_{f=1}^d s_f^2}{\frac{\bar k}{d}\left(1-\frac{\bar k}{d}\right)}, \qquad s_f^2 = \frac{F}{F-1}\hat p_f(1-\hat p_f).
\]
% TODO: manual review
Here $\hat p_f$ is the fraction of folds that selected feature $f$, and $\bar k$ is the mean number of features selected. $\hat\Phi=1$ means the same features on every fold, and $\hat\Phi \approx 0$ is what random selection gives. Each fold picks its own budget, and the index allows for the resulting difference in set size through $\bar k$. To read a blueprint, each selected feature is traced to its base representation, and its importance in the fitted model is measured with SHAP: TreeSHAP for tree models \citep{Lundberg2020}, the DeepLIFT-based SHAP explainer for the DNN, and Kernel SHAP on 50 held-out samples for the SVM and the Gaussian process \citep{Lundberg2017}.
