Federated learning trains one model across data held by several parties without moving the data \citep{McMahan2017}. It has been run across pharmaceutical companies \citep{Heyndrickx2024}, whose structures and assay results are commercial assets that cannot be pooled \citep{Hanser2023,Bassani2023}. Our collaborator, Lhasa Limited, uses the teacher-student process of Chapter~\ref{chap:background} (Table~\ref{tab:noise_sources}) \citep{Hanser2025}. We use their code for the fingerprints, site models and site clustering, and re-implement from it the step that combines the site models' predictions.

% TODO: manual review
Our addition is that feature selection, as well as model fitting, takes place at the sites. One search on the training molecules of all sites together chooses the settings (allocation method, importance method and internal evaluator). Each site then selects features with these settings on its own molecules and reports only feature indices, importance values and one score. A coordinator splits the total budget $B$ across sites in proportion to their scores, with at least one feature for each site whose selection succeeded. Only this proportional split is run, so we cannot say what the site scores buy. A feature already taken by another site is replaced by the site's next-ranked feature, which keeps all features in the hybrid distinct. Indices and importances are smaller, and harder to invert, than model weights or gradients. In related work, each client runs mutual-information feature selection on its own data and a server aggregates the results \citep{Cassara2022}.

% TODO: manual review
All four separate-site experiments run on Caco-2 and LogD, with five folds grouped by scaffold family and three seeds, the DNN and SVM, and budgets of 500 to 2{,}000 features. In the main one, each site holds the same molecules but one family of representations: substructure fingerprints, physicochemical descriptors or pretrained embeddings. It compares the best single family, picked on the training molecules, with one feature selection over all families, and both with one selection per family at an even share of the budget. When a family is narrower than its share, the rest goes to the other families. The even split can, as a result, build fewer features than one selection over all families. The built widths are recorded on every fold.

