With the same downstream model on every representation, an ensemble of one model per representation beats the hybrid with both models on most antibody datasets with a result (Table~\ref{tab:method:ensembles}). On the anti-VEGF expression scan the DNN ensemble is ahead of the hybrid on every fold. On small molecules the hybrid holds up better: the same-model ensemble beats it with both models only on hERG blocking, and on the Buchwald--Hartwig reactions. The hybrid is ahead of both ensembles on QM9 with the DNN and on hERG $K_i$ with the SVM, on every fold. On antibodies the representations are language models of one sequence that make similar predictions, and averaging them smooths out the noise of each model on a small training set. On small molecules the representations carry different information, and one model on their selected features can use interactions that an average of separate models never sees.

