Figure~\ref{fig:best_number_of_features} and Additional file~\ref{app:add:feature_reduction} compare the hybrid with recursive feature elimination and random features over all base representations joined. Recursive elimination ranks all joined features in one list, the pooled ranking the hybrid avoids (Section~\ref{sec:method:searchspace}). On QM9, Caco-2, LogD and MDR1--MDCK efflux the hybrid has the lower error at every budget with both models. The exception is hERG $K_i$ with the DNN, where recursive elimination and even random features beat the hybrid at 1,000 and 2,000 features. On the Buchwald--Hartwig reactions recursive elimination is ahead at most budgets. On antibodies (Figure~\ref{fig:best_number_of_features_antibodies}) random features are beaten at most budgets, but recursive elimination is ahead on $\Delta\Delta G$ with the SVM and on CHO polyreactivity at every budget.

