Figures~\ref{fig:best_number_of_features} and~\ref{fig:best_number_of_features_antibodies} show the budget each fold's search picked, and Additional file~\ref{app:add:budget_per_fold} lists it. We have not yet run the experiment that tests whether the best settings follow measurable properties of a dataset, so we only describe these picks. Much of what looks like a dataset pattern was fixed in advance by each dataset's grid (Table~\ref{tab:decided_grid}). The QM9, LogD and hERG blocking searches could use only LightGBM gain. SMAC colloidal stability, HIC retention, expression titer and CHO polyreactivity searched only budgets wider than their 152 to 198 training antibodies. Where the grid left a choice, the downstream model moved the pick as much as the dataset did. Dataset size did not order the budgets either: QM9, the largest dataset with 7,982 training molecules per fold, picked 1,000 features on 4 of 5 folds with the DNN, below LogD's 2,000.

% TODO: manual review
On Caco-2 the search picked more features for the DNN than for the SVM, and on nanobody thermostability it picked 1,000 features for the DNN on four of five folds against 250 or 500 for the SVM (Figure~\ref{fig:best_number_of_features_antibodies}). On LogD it picked 2,000 for the DNN on every fold, the top of the grid, so the best width for that model may lie above what was searched. The SVM's smaller picks fit what Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} showed for distances: a kernel computed over many features is diluted by the ones that carry nothing, while a network can learn to ignore them. On angiopoietin-2 and $\Delta\Delta G$ the search picks at most a few hundred features. On expression titer the random forest's pick varies widely between folds, the selection noise of the previous section showing up in the budget. No one budget or representation count would serve every dataset and model, which is why both are searched.

