\section{Conclusions}

% TODO: manual review
This chapter asked how the best set of features can be selected for a given prediction task (Section~\ref{sec:intro:hybrid_questions}). We found that the answer depends on the dataset and the downstream model, and that a search can find it. On every small-molecule dataset the hybrid beats the best single representation with both downstream models, and it beats even the representation that was best in hindsight. It does so because the representations carry different information: on hERG $K_i$, Caco-2 and MDR1--MDCK efflux the hybrids draw on five to seven of them about equally. On small molecules this supports the hypothesis behind the hybrid, that the feature set is part of the problem.

% TODO: manual review
At the same number of features the hybrid beats recursive elimination and random features on all but one small-molecule dataset with a result. Its gain is therefore more than a reduction in width. Drawing on several representations beats drawing on the top one alone on most datasets, and which representations are drawn on matters more than how the budget is split. Since we found no setting that suits every dataset and model, the settings should be searched for each new task rather than fixed in advance. The DNN picked more features than the SVM on Caco-2 and LogD, and cheaper internal evaluators were good enough on small molecules but not on some antibody datasets. Finally, the selected features can be read: on LogD the hybrid picks lipophilicity and basicity on every fold, from two different representations.

% TODO: manual review
On antibodies the hybrid helps for the same reason it helps on small molecules: the features that matter are few and buried in representations of the whole sequence. On $\Delta\Delta G$, AC-SINS self-association and the anti-VEGF expression scan it beats every single representation. On the first and last of these, the best single representation is one we built to read the regions and mutations that vary. On the smaller sets, however, an ensemble of one model per representation often does better. On reactions the method gains nothing on the additive split. On data held at separate sites, selecting at each site does as well as one selection over all representations. The hybrid can likewise take in new embeddings added to the pool.

% TODO: manual review
The fourth question asked whether weighting features by their importance improves the hybrid and model training. In construction, ranking features by importance within each representation is what makes the hybrid work. In training, by contrast, using the importance scores again adds little on small molecules and helps on some small antibody datasets, with no condition that works everywhere.

% TODO: manual review
\label{sec:method:limitations}
% TODO: manual review
The hybrid needs data. Its gains are consistent on datasets of a few thousand samples and inconsistent on a few hundred, where the search's own scores overstate what the hybrid delivers (Section~\ref{sec:method:results_overfit}). Most of our antibody datasets are that small, so their results say as much about dataset size as about antibodies. The antibody pools also offer few kinds of representation, mostly language models of the same sequence. The search is costly and must be rerun when the data, representations or model change. Where a simpler selection, such as recursive elimination or a tuned model on a wider hybrid, does as well, the search costs more than it returns. The separate sites are simulated from one public dataset.

% TODO: manual review
This chapter used the hybrid only as input to a model. Whether hybrids keep more of their performance under label noise, and whether their features stabilize noise mitigation, are the subjects of Chapters~\ref{chap:similarity} and~\ref{chap:noise}. Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity} first asks whether a feature set chosen for the task also gives better neighbors, which every method that relies on similarity could use, including the detection of noisy labels.

\chapter{Similarity in an adaptive hybrid representation}\label{chap:similarity}

\section{Introduction}

% TODO: manual review
Chapter~\ref{chap:method} built the adaptive hybrid representation, a set of features selected from several base representations for one task, as input to a model. Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} showed that in most standard representations the distance between two molecules barely rises with the difference in their labels. Any method that trusts a molecule's neighbors inherits this, such as predicting a label from the nearest neighbors or finding a noisy label by comparing it with its neighbors' labels. The first idea behind the hybrid was to compute distances on the features relevant to the task alone, and this chapter tests that idea.

% TODO: manual review
A hybrid can be built for similarity in two ways. In the first, the search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} uses a nearest-neighbor model as its downstream model, and we measure distances in the hybrid it selects. In the second, the search ranks each candidate hybrid by how well each molecule's five nearest neighbors in it predict its label, and fits no downstream model. We ask two research questions:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Does distance in a hybrid track label differences better than distance in the best single representation?
    \item Do the methods built on that distance work better in a hybrid? We test prediction from nearest neighbors, activity cliffs, Bayesian optimization, distance to the training molecules as a guide to prediction error, retrieval of neighbors and clustering.
\end{enumerate}

% TODO: manual review
This chapter uses 14 datasets: six small-molecule, seven antibody and one reaction dataset. Antibody datasets outnumber small-molecule datasets, so we give every result by domain rather than pooled.

