Datasets, folds (grouped by scaffold family for small molecules) and metrics are as in Chapter~\ref{chap:method}, with five outer folds. Some experiments have no result on some datasets because their runs did not finish in time (Section~\ref{sec:method:evaluation}), and Section~\ref{sec:similarity:conclusions} lists them. MDR1--MDCK efflux was added late and has no row in the tables of distance in the predictive hybrids and in each feature space, the best number of features, importance weighting in the four models, and activity cliffs. In each outer fold, everything an experiment compares is chosen from searches on that fold's training molecules alone, and then scored on its held-out molecules. Features are scaled on the training molecules: features that only take the values 0 and 1 are left as they are, and the rest are standardized. In the experiments, but not inside the searches, held-out values are cut to the training range as well, which stops a single extreme molecule from dominating a distance. Where a statement covers all 13 datasets, ``molecule'' also stands for an antibody or a reaction.

% TODO: manual review
The fold split, the sample of molecule pairs, the label permutations, the random feature subsets and the k-means starts all use fixed seeds. The spread we report is therefore the spread across the five outer folds of one split. We report the mean over the five folds, and the per-fold values where the spread matters, without significance tests. The simulation of choosing molecules to measure is repeated over three starting sets instead.

\subsection{Feature spaces compared}\label{sec:similarity:spaces}

% TODO: manual review
Each outer fold runs three searches on its training molecules. The first, the general search, is the search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}, run for each of the dataset's two downstream models (Table~\ref{tab:decided_grid}). The second, the distance search, uses a k-nearest-neighbor model as its downstream model, with Euclidean distance and five equally weighted neighbors, or fewer when a fold has under 25 training molecules. It has its own lists of importance methods, allocation methods, numbers of representations and budgets. Its budgets are capped at 500 features, and on some datasets its other lists differ from the general search's too. On hERG $K_i$, for example, the general search tries 250, 500, 1,000 and 2,000 features, while the distance search tries 100, 250 and 500. The third is the similarity search, described in Section~\ref{sec:similarity:hybrid_similarity}. From these three searches, we take the following feature spaces:
\begin{itemize}
    \item the nearest-neighbor model's best and second-best single representations, ranked by the distance search;
    \item each downstream model's best and second-best single representations, ranked by its general search;
    \item each downstream model's two best hybrids, four in all: the predictive hybrids;
    \item the top hybrid of the distance search: the distance hybrid;
    \item the top hybrid of the similarity search: the similarity hybrid, on the datasets where that search is defined.
\end{itemize}

% TODO: manual review
Unless stated otherwise, ``the single representation'' in the results means the nearest-neighbor model's best single representation, and ``the hybrid'' means the distance hybrid. Retrieval and clustering also score one control: the principal components of all representations joined, with as many components as the hybrid has features.

\subsection{Similarity in a hybrid}\label{sec:similarity:hybrid_similarity}

% TODO: manual review
The search of Chapter~\ref{chap:method} can be written as follows. Each configuration $\theta$ fixes an importance method, an allocation method, a number of representations and a budget $B$. Given base representations $R_1, \dots, R_m$, a configuration builds a feature set $S_\theta \subset R_1 \cup \dots \cup R_m$ with $|S_\theta| \le B$. The search returns $S_{\theta^*}$, where $\theta^* = \arg\max_\theta s(S_\theta)$ and $s$ is the search's score. In the general search, $s$ is a downstream model's score on the search's own held-out folds. In the similarity search, $s(S) = q_5(S)$, how accurately each molecule's label is predicted from its five nearest neighbors in $S$ (Equation~\ref{eq:similarity_objective}), and no downstream model is fitted. All other settings and the folds are shared with the general search. The neighbor score, computed on a validation set taken from the fold's training molecules, also replaces the internal evaluator when the budget is split.

% TODO: manual review
\citet{Zheng2000} and \citet{Itskowitz2005} chose descriptors to maximize nearest-neighbor prediction within one descriptor set, and we modify this by choosing features across several representations. The similarity hybrid tests whether choosing features for how well the neighbors predict a label does better than choosing them for a model's prediction.

% TODO: manual review
The score uses the Manhattan distance of Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}, on features scaled as above. Each standardized feature adds a term of similar size to this distance. A representation's weight in the distance, which the allocation method sets, is thus roughly its number of features divided by the budget. A fingerprint weighs less than this per feature, because a feature that only takes the values 0 and 1 adds less. The neighbor score uses no importance weighting. We chose Manhattan distance in an earlier comparison of four distances on hERG $K_i$ and LogD, over seven representations each. It was the only distance never much worse than second on any of three scores: splitting the budget, predicting from neighbors and agreeing with shared structure, although cosine was better at predicting from neighbors. The inverse-distance weights were not compared when the score was built. A later comparison on four datasets used Manhattan distance with 5 neighbors. There, inverse-distance weights beat equal weights in all 48 pairs of dataset and representation, but lost to inverse squared distance in 39 of the 48.

% TODO: manual review
To rank whole candidates, we compute the score on the held-out molecules of each fold of the search, inside the outer training fold. These molecules act as each other's neighbors. Their groups (scaffold families, for small molecules) are split into up to 5 parts, and each molecule's 5 nearest neighbors are drawn from the other parts. A neighbor from the same scaffold family is an easy match and would inflate the score. This is the only place in the chapter where a molecule's own group is kept out of its neighbors. Each neighbor is weighted by the inverse of its distance \citep{Dudani1976}:
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}_i = \frac{\sum_{j\in N_5(i)} y_j / d_{ij}}{\sum_{j\in N_5(i)} 1/d_{ij}}.
\label{eq:similarity_objective}
\end{equation}
% TODO: manual review
A neighbor at distance 0 receives all of the weight, shared equally if there are several. On regression data, the score $q_5$ is the Spearman correlation $\rho(\hat{\mathbf{y}}, \mathbf{y})$. On binary data, $\hat{y}_i$ is the distance-weighted fraction of neighbors in class 1, and $q_5 = 2(\mathrm{AUROC} - 0.5)$, where AUROC is the area under the receiver operating characteristic curve. Both scores put chance at 0, which the budget split needs, because each representation's share of the budget is its score divided by the sum of the scores. The modelability index of \citet{Golbraikh2014} (Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws}) is related, but uses a single nearest neighbor and a hard class match.

% TODO: manual review
A fold of the search is undefined when it has fewer than 10 held-out molecules or fewer than 2 groups, or when its labels or predictions are constant. A candidate hybrid with an undefined fold is refused. We do not run the similarity search where its score is undefined:
\begin{itemize}
    \item The four Ginkgo developability datasets in this chapter (AC-SINS self-association, SMAC colloidal stability, CHO polyreactivity and expression titer) use the competition's five folds as their five groups. Inside an outer fold, each held-out block of the search is then a single group, so no antibody has a neighbor outside its own group.
    \item On the change in binding free energy ($\Delta\Delta G$) caused by a mutation, one protein complex holds 69 of the 429 rows. On outer folds 1 to 4 it falls alone into one held-out block of the search, and every candidate is refused. We skip this dataset on all five folds.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Nearest-neighbor settings}\label{sec:similarity:knn_settings}

% TODO: manual review
Every experiment that predicts from neighbors or measures retrieval scores each feature space at every combination of the settings in Table~\ref{tab:knn_settings}, instead of at one chosen setting. A held-out molecule's prediction is the weighted mean of its neighbors' labels,
\begin{equation}
\hat{y}_i = \frac{\sum_{j \in N_k(i)} w_{ij}\, y_j}{\sum_{j \in N_k(i)} w_{ij}},
\label{eq:knn_weights}
\end{equation}
where $N_k(i)$ is the $k$ training molecules nearest to $i$ under distance $d$, and $w_{ij}$ is the neighbor weight. On classification data, each class receives the summed weight of its neighbors. A molecule's own group (scaffold family, for small molecules) is never excluded from its neighbors, only the molecule itself. The one experiment that does not use these settings is the one on all representations joined (Section~\ref{sec:similarity:experiments}).

