\subsubsection{Distance against label difference}
% TODO: manual review
For one feature space, this experiment estimates the Spearman correlation between the distance of two held-out molecules and the absolute difference in their labels. A positive value means that molecules further apart differ more in their labels. We estimate it in each outer fold from a sample of up to 50,000 pairs. We measure it first in each of the dataset's representations alone, under every distance of Table~\ref{tab:knn_settings}, with no selection and no importance weighting. We then measure it in the feature spaces of Section~\ref{sec:similarity:spaces}, unweighted and weighted.

\subsubsection{Prediction from nearest neighbors}
% TODO: manual review
We predict each held-out molecule's label from its nearest training molecules (Equation~\ref{eq:knn_weights}), and make two comparisons. The first was fixed before the run, and nothing in it is picked on the folds it is scored on. It compares the distance hybrid against the single representation, both unweighted, under Manhattan distance with 5 neighbors weighted by the inverse of their rank. The second picks a setting again for each held-out fold, using the other four folds' held-out scores. On the hybrid side, it picks among up to six hybrids (the four predictive hybrids, the distance hybrid and the similarity hybrid), weighted and unweighted, at every setting of Table~\ref{tab:knn_settings}, up to 216 combinations. On the single side, it picks among the nearest-neighbor model's two single representations, unweighted only, which gives 36. On the three datasets with two distances, the numbers are 144 and 24, and where the similarity hybrid is undefined they are 180 and 36. So the two sides differ in two ways at once. The hybrid side picks from five or six times as many combinations, and only the hybrid side can use importance weighting.

\subsubsection{Importance weighting in four models}
% TODO: manual review
We score every feature space with and without importance weighting, using four models that read a distance. The first three are prediction from nearest neighbors at every setting, a Gaussian process with an RBF kernel and a support vector regressor with an RBF kernel. The fourth is each downstream model given four extra features from its nearest training molecules, following q-RASAR \citep{Banerjee2022}. The four extra features are the neighbors' mean label and its spread, the distance to the nearest neighbor and the mean distance to the neighbors. They are computed at the three neighbor settings that rank best in a screen on the training molecules. The Gaussian process and the support vector regressor run on regression data only. A separate experiment applies the weighting to all representations joined, on regression datasets, once for each importance method.

\subsubsection{Activity cliffs}
% TODO: manual review
An activity cliff is a pair of structurally similar molecules with very different activity \citep{Stumpfe2012,vanTilborg2022}. Only the two hERG datasets measure an activity, but we keep the term for the other three small-molecule datasets. We decide which molecules are cliff molecules in each outer fold, from its training molecules. We keep the structural side of the rule of \citet{Hu2015}: two molecules are similar when their ECFP4 Tanimoto similarity is at least 0.55. We modify its activity side, because a gap of two log units flags no molecule at all on Caco-2. Instead, we choose the gap so that 15\% of the training molecules are cliff molecules. A training molecule is a cliff molecule when it is in at least one similar pair whose labels differ by more than the gap. On classification data, a similar pair counts when its two molecules are in different classes. A held-out molecule is a cliff molecule when it has at least two similar training molecules whose labels span more than that gap; its own label is never read. Each downstream model is fitted on its two best single representations and its two best hybrids. Each fit is scored separately on the cliff molecules and on the other held-out molecules. The main comparison is the first hybrid against the best single representation.

\subsubsection{Distance against prediction error}
% TODO: manual review
This follows \citet{Sheridan2004}, who found that a molecule's similarity to the training molecules tracks how well it is predicted. Each downstream model is fitted twice, on all representations joined and on its own first hybrid, and we record its error on every held-out molecule. On regression data this is the absolute error, and on classification data it is one minus the predicted probability of the true class. We then compute the Spearman correlation between that error and the molecule's mean distance to its nearest training molecules, at 5, 10 and 20 neighbors and every distance. We do this in each feature space of Section~\ref{sec:similarity:spaces}, unweighted and weighted.

\subsubsection{Retrieval and clustering}
% TODO: manual review
Retrieval measures how close a molecule's neighbors are to it in label. For each held-out molecule, the label gap (lower is better) is the weighted mean absolute difference between its label and those of its nearest training molecules, at every setting of Table~\ref{tab:knn_settings}. On classification data, it is one minus the weighted share of neighbors in the molecule's class. For clustering, we fit scikit-learn's k-means \citep{Lloyd1982} on the training molecules with 3, 5 and 10 clusters, and assign each held-out molecule to a cluster. Label coherence (higher is better) is one minus the mean standard deviation of the held-out labels within a cluster, divided by the standard deviation of all held-out labels. On classification data, it is the mean share of the largest class in each cluster. Clusters with fewer than two held-out molecules are left out.

\subsubsection{Three smaller checks}
% TODO: manual review
% TODO(version): the scikit-learn version used on the server. Local is 1.6.1; not checked on the server.
To find the best number of features, we take each downstream model's top hybrid at every budget of its search. At each budget we record, for that same hybrid, its prediction score and the correlation between distance and label difference, and find the budget where each peaks. On hERG $K_i$ and Buchwald--Hartwig only, we simulate choosing which molecules to measure, a form of Bayesian optimization. Starting from a set of whole scaffold families holding at least 5\% of the molecules, a surrogate model picks 100 molecules one at a time by expected improvement \citep{Jones1998}. We repeat this from three starting sets, and the score is the area under the best-found-so-far curve. Last, for each representation a predictive hybrid draws from, we compare the label information in the features it kept against a random subset of the same size. Each block of features is reduced to at most 50 principal components. A mutual-information estimate is summed over the components, and the mean over three label permutations is subtracted. We use scikit-learn's estimators, which follow \citet{Kraskov2004} for continuous labels and \citet{Ross2014} for classes. This sum counts information shared by two components twice.

