\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:similarity:conclusions}

% TODO: manual review
Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} found that distance in standard representations barely rises with label difference. On the six small-molecule datasets, we found that distance in a hybrid built for distance tracks label differences more closely than in the single representation. Prediction from nearest neighbors improves on all six, both at the setting fixed before the run and with the setting picked again. In addition, the neighbors retrieved in the hybrid have closer labels. On LogD, Caco-2, hERG $K_i$ and hERG blocking, each model's first hybrid also predicts cliff molecules better than its best single representation.

% TODO: manual review
Weighting features by importance does part of the same work without any selection. On LogD it lets the single representation track label differences better than the unweighted hybrid. The hybrid's distance does, however, track prediction error worse on hERG blocking, and except on QM9 and Buchwald--Hartwig its kept features carry no more label information than principal components of the same size.

% TODO: manual review
On the antibody datasets and Buchwald--Hartwig, distance in the hybrid also tracks label differences more closely, but the methods built on it do not always gain. With the setting picked again, prediction from nearest neighbors is worse in the hybrid on nanobody thermostability, AC-SINS self-association, CHO polyreactivity, expression titer and Buchwald--Hartwig. Retrieval is worse on $\Delta\Delta G$ and Buchwald--Hartwig. These losses agree with the central claim of this thesis: the right input depends on the data, so we must measure a hybrid's benefit on each dataset.

