The gap over neighbor spread is the buddy check used to screen weather-station readings, which divides a reading's gap from its neighbors' mean by their standard deviation \citep{Baserud2020}. RegENN applies a similar threshold to filter noisy regression labels \citep{Kordos2012}, but we found no earlier use of either on molecular labels. The cliff-aware gap divides by the spread of all labels plus that of the neighbors. A molecule among neighbors that disagree with each other, as near an activity cliff, scores lower as a result. SLOM lowers an outlier score in the same way where a neighborhood is unstable, but for spatial data, where neighbors are found by location \citep{Chawla2006}.

% TODO: manual review
The variance-head network predicts a mean $\mu(x)$ and a variance $\sigma^2(x)$ and is trained with
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = \frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{(y - \mu)^2}{\sigma^2} + \log \sigma^2\right) \cdot \sigma^{2\beta},
\label{eq:beta_nll}
\end{equation}
where $\beta = 0.5$ keeps the variance from hiding the errors in the mean \citep{Seitzer2022}.

% TODO: manual review
A neighbor detector needs a space to find neighbors in, a neighbor count $k$ and a way to weight the neighbors. We try $k$ of 5, 10 and 20, and weight each neighbor by $1/\text{rank}$ or by $1/d^2$. The setting is chosen inside each fold, without the held-out molecules. We hold back 20\% of the training scaffold families and predict each held-back label as the weighted mean of its neighbors' labels among the other training molecules. The three settings that predict best are kept. The space is a single representation, a hybrid, or one of the similarity spaces of Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}.

% TODO: manual review
Two experiments change the space itself. The first multiplies each z-scored feature by the square root of its importance, rescaled so that the multipliers average 1, before the neighbors are found. The importance is computed against the damaged training labels, as feature-weighted nearest neighbors do \citep{Wettschereck1997}. The second compares four families of distance for the neighbor detectors. They are the plain features under Euclidean and Manhattan distance, the features weighted by importance raised to 0.5 or 1.0, forest proximity, and the hybrid's own features. The forest proximity of two molecules is the share of trees in which they fall in the same leaf \citep{Liaw2002}, and their distance is one minus it. The forest has 100 trees with at least 5 molecules per leaf, fitted to the fold's damaged training labels.

% TODO: manual review
One experiment compares several further detectors with the out-of-fold residual. One is the value of each molecule to a nearest-neighbor predictor, as a Shapley value \citep{ghorbani2019data, jia2019efficient}. Another is the cleanlab regression score \citep{zhou2024detecting}, which draws its internal folds at random rather than by scaffold family. The rest divide the residual by a spread for that molecule, from NGBoost, a quantile forest or a conformal interval.

% TODO: manual review
Two experiments test the out-of-fold LightGBM residual under the shift in 10\% sized by model error. The first ranks molecules by the residual itself. The second divides each residual by a second LightGBM model's out-of-fold estimate of how large that molecule's residual usually is. It then turns the result into conformal $p$-values against a calibration set of training molecules \citep{bates2023testing}. Molecules are flagged with the Benjamini--Hochberg rule \citep{benjamini1995controlling}, which bounds the expected share of false flags at 5\%, 10\% or 20\%. The calibration set is a random 20\% or 50\% of the training molecules or, where a dataset has repeat measurements, the molecules with three or more measurements that agree. Both experiments grade the ranking by the area under the ROC curve.

% TODO: manual review
A detector can be graded only where the damage moved some labels and not others, so that it is known which molecules were damaged. That is outlier noise, censoring, the two shifts in 10\% and every label flip. Under Gaussian and grouped-shifted noise every label moves, and no detection score exists. We grade a detector by the average precision of its suspicion scores against the set of damaged molecules. A detector that ranks at random scores the damaged share of the training molecules; that share is the chance level. Some experiments report the area under the ROC curve instead, where chance is 0.5.

\subsection{Noise mitigation}\label{sec:noise:mitigation}

% TODO: manual review
Table~\ref{tab:noise:mitigation} describes each noise mitigation method by its two halves (Table~\ref{tab:noise_terms}). The methods reduce to a few selectors crossed with three actions.

% TODO: manual review
Most methods read the suspicion score rescaled to $[0, 1]$, $\tilde s_i = (s_i - \min s)/(\max s - \min s)$. Deleting drops the molecules above a quantile of $s$ (Table~\ref{tab:noise:mitigation}). This is the filtering of \citet{Brodley1999}, which \citet{Zhao2017} tested on QSAR modeling sets. Down-weighting gives molecule $i$ the weight $1/(1 + \alpha\tilde s_i)$, and all weights are then divided by their mean. A proportional move takes the label part of the way to a target $t_i$,
\begin{equation}
y_i' = y_i + \alpha\,\tilde s_i\,(t_i - y_i),
\label{eq:noise:move}
\end{equation}
so the most suspect label moves a share $\alpha$ of the way and the least suspect does not move. The target is the weighted mean of the molecule's neighbors' labels \citep{nadaraya1964estimating, watson1964smooth}, or an out-of-fold LightGBM prediction, which is close to the target blending of \citet{reed2014training}. One further rule, the score-scaled shrink, moves a share $0.5\tilde s_i/(1 + 0.5\tilde s_i)$ of the way to the out-of-fold prediction. It was written as James--Stein shrinkage \citep{james1961estimation}. The variance it estimates cancels out of the share, so the share depends only on the score.

