\subsubsection{Selected by a detector}
% TODO: manual review
The main cleanup experiment takes the out-of-fold forest residual as its detector and crosses it with each action above. The cliff-aware gap is graded as a detector beside it. A second experiment asks whether to delete a flagged molecule or keep it, and keeps it either with a lower weight or with a moved label. Its control deletes the same number of molecules at random, at five seeds. A third experiment replaces the forest residual with each of five detectors in turn: the cliff-aware gap, and the GP, the heteroscedastic GP, the variance-head network and NGBoost. Each deletes its top 5\%, 10\% or 20\%. Where the detector has its own prediction, it also moves every label toward that prediction. The share moved is given by Equation~\ref{eq:noise:move}, or by $c_i = \alpha s_i/(1 + \alpha s_i)$, which uses the score in its own units.

% TODO: manual review
Four experiments weight molecules by a neighbor or model detector, with no deletion. Three use the weight $1/(1 + 3\tilde s_i)$, under which the most suspect molecule counts a quarter as much as the least suspect. They differ in where the neighbors are found: in features scaled by importance on clean labels, in the importance-scaled space above, and in each of the four families of distance. The fourth uses $\exp(-0.1\,\tilde s_i)$ with the gap over neighbor spread, the GP or NGBoost as the detector. With that weight, the most suspect molecule counts about 0.9 times as much as the least suspect.

% TODO: manual review
Close-pair weights look only at pairs of near-identical molecules. A pair is close if its distance is in the lowest 1\% of all training pairs. The label noise is estimated from the closest fifth of those pairs as $\hat\sigma^2 = \overline{\Delta y^2}/2$, since two noisy copies of one value differ by twice the noise variance. Both molecules of any close pair whose labels differ by more than $3\sqrt{2}\,\hat\sigma$ get weight 0.25. Residual flags give weight 0.25 to the top 5\%, 10\% or 20\% of molecules by out-of-fold LightGBM residual. The same experiment also asks how precise a detector has to be before acting on its flags helps. It flags sets of molecules of the same size with a known share of truly damaged molecules, from none to all, and finds the share at which the gain from acting crosses zero. We call that share the break-even precision.

% TODO: manual review
Cliff put-back runs after a cleanup and undoes it on activity-cliff molecules: deleted molecules return, lowered weights go back to 1 and moved labels go back to their training value. A training molecule is a cliff molecule if it has a partner with ECFP4 Tanimoto similarity of at least 0.55 \citep{Hu2015} whose label is far from its own \citep{Maggiora2006, Stumpfe2012}. Far means more than twice the median absolute deviation of the labels. On datasets whose label is not a biological activity, such as LogD, the same test finds pairs that differ sharply in that property, and we keep the name. The cliff set is rebuilt in every fold from the damaged training labels. Its control puts back the same number of molecules drawn at random from the non-cliff molecules. We found no earlier method that puts examples back after a cleanup. Some cleaning methods exempt a whole class decided in advance \citep{Kubat1997, Stefanowski2008}, and \citet{Brodley1999} name the risk of discarding correct exceptions without giving a rule.

% TODO: manual review
The hybrid's features are chosen against the labels, which means the labels can be cleaned before the features are chosen or after. One experiment compares the two orders, with nine cleanups that use the out-of-fold forest residual as the detector, under outlier noise and on labels as delivered. The search and the fit each use the labels as given or the cleaned labels, four combinations in all. Down-weighting cannot reach the search, because no weight enters the feature ranking or the budget split. Its two cleaned-search arms consequently do not exist. No source we found crosses features chosen on cleaned or recorded labels with a fit on cleaned or recorded labels. The nearest design ranks features on one version of the data and fits on another, but its second step is class-imbalance sampling, not label cleaning \citep{Shanab2012}.

% TODO: manual review
On labels as delivered, nothing says which molecules are wrong. A gain can come from removing hard molecules rather than wrong ones. One experiment runs seven cleanups on delivered labels with the neighbor gap and the forest residual as detectors. Its control shuffles each detector's scores across the training molecules, at three seeds, and reruns the cleanup with the same share and strength. The same number of molecules is touched, but not the ones the detector picked.

\subsubsection{Selected by a known flag}\label{sec:noise:censoring}
% TODO: manual review
When a label is clipped at an assay limit, we know two things that a detector has to guess: which labels are wrong, and in which direction. The limit $L$ is read off the clipped training labels as their largest value, if at least two molecules and 1\% of the training molecules sit on it. An out-of-fold LightGBM fit to the clipped labels gives each molecule a prediction $p_i$. Let $\sigma$ be the standard deviation of $y_i - p_i$ over the molecules below the limit, and $m_i$ the inverse Mills ratio,
\begin{equation}
m_i = \frac{\phi(a_i)}{1 - \Phi(a_i)}, \qquad a_i = \frac{L - p_i}{\sigma},
\end{equation}
% TODO: citation check 2026-10-10 -- Tobin1958 gives the censored-normal likelihood, not the replacement step.
where $\phi$ and $\Phi$ are the standard normal density and distribution function. The truncated-normal mean replaces each clipped label by the mean of a normal distribution centered on its prediction and cut at the limit, following the censored-normal model of \citet{Tobin1958},
\begin{equation}
y_i' = \max\!\left(L,\; p_i + \sigma m_i\right).
\label{eq:noise:truncated}
\end{equation}
Censored EM repeats this for three rounds, refitting the downstream model out of fold to the current labels each round, after the iterated estimator of \citet{buckley1979linear}. The constant lift raises every clipped label to $L + \overline{\sigma m}$, the mean of $\sigma m_i$ over the clipped molecules. That shared amount is larger than the truncated-normal mean's lift by $L - p_i$ on average. Two arms test what the flag is worth. Deleting the clipped molecules uses the flag but throws the molecules away. A per-molecule lift raises each clipped label by its own $\sigma m_i$, and its control deals the same lifts to the wrong clipped molecules, at three seeds. \citet{Svensson2025} fitted censored regression to drug-discovery labels, which is the closest precedent.

% TODO: manual review
A scaffold family is also a known flag. Under grouped-shifted noise every family carries its own offset. A family's mean out-of-fold residual estimates that offset, and subtracting it from every label in the family undoes it. Three versions differ in how much they trust a small family. The first subtracts the whole mean residual from every family, and the second only from families of three or more training molecules. The third is a random-intercept model, fitted by up to five rounds of expectation maximization in the style of mixed-effects random forests \citep{hajjem2014mixed, sigrist2022gaussian}. It shrinks each family's offset toward zero, more for a family with few molecules.

% TODO: manual review
A source is a known flag when each molecule was measured by several of them. One experiment simulates eight sources, and each molecule is measured by one to four of them, drawn from hERG IC$_{50}$'s real counts or uniformly. Measurement $s$ of molecule $i$ is
\begin{equation}
y_{is} = y_i + b_s + \sigma_e\left(\sqrt{\rho}\,u_i + \sqrt{1-\rho}\,v_{is}\right),
\end{equation}
where $b_s$ is the source's offset, $u_i$ is an error shared by every measurement of the molecule and $v_{is}$ is an error of its own. The value of $\rho$, 0, 0.3 or 0.7, sets how much of the error the measurements share. The offsets have standard deviation 0 or $0.5\,\sigma_e$. The training label is the mean of the molecule's measurements. The methods remove each source's offset, estimated by a mixed model with the source as a fixed effect and the molecule as a random intercept. They also weight each molecule by the inverse of its estimated error variance \citep{hedges1985meta}. Two further arms use the true $\rho$ and $\sigma_e$, which a user never has; hence they are bounds and not methods.

% TODO: manual review
hERG IC$_{50}$ carries its own known flag: how many times each molecule was measured, and how far apart those measurements are. A molecule is one canonical SMILES string as given by ChEMBL. Its count is its number of ChEMBL IC$_{50}$ records, and its spread is the variance of their pIC$_{50}$ values. Of the three methods that use the flag, the first weights each molecule by its count. The second weights it by the inverse of its estimated error variance, $1/(\sigma_u^2 + \tilde s_i^2/n_i)$ \citep{hedges1985meta}. Here $n_i$ is the number of measurements behind the label, and $\sigma_u^2$ is the error that remains however often a molecule is measured. Its spread $\tilde s_i^2$ is pulled toward the spread pooled over all molecules, as if the pool were three more measurements. We do this because a variance from two or three records is unreliable \citep{smyth2004limma}. The third moves each label toward an out-of-fold LightGBM prediction, further for a less precise label. The share moved is $v_i/(v_i + \tau^2)$, where $v_i$ is the molecule's spread over its count and $\tau^2$ is the variance of the out-of-fold residual. Held-out molecules are graded only if they have three or more measurements.

