QM9 separates the two things a gain can mean (Section~\ref{sec:noise:tests}). A method that removes noise should leave QM9's computed labels alone and improve measured labels as delivered. A method that also helps QM9 is, at least in part, a better way to train. We apply this test to each kind of method here, before the sections that go through them one by one.

% TODO: manual review
Deletion passes the test. On QM9 with no noise added it made every model worse, in every configuration (Table~\ref{tab:noise:res:qm9_deletion}). Deleting 20\% of the molecules flagged by the forest residual does the same on QM9's labels (Table~\ref{tab:noise:res:shuffled}). On SMAC colloidal stability and expression titer, the same deletion improved the labels as delivered and beat every shuffled control. These are the clearest cases in the chapter of a method finding noise that was already in the data. On the small-molecule labels as delivered, deletion found nothing to remove (Section~\ref{sec:noise:res:delivered}).

% TODO: manual review
Label smoothing, weighting and moving labels toward their neighbors pass the test only in part. With the network they improve QM9 with no noise added (Tables~\ref{tab:noise:res:weighting} and~\ref{tab:noise:res:shuffled}, and the QM9 row of Figure~\ref{fig:noise:did_cleaning_work_detector}). LightGBM, which gives the same score on every fit, barely moves on QM9. On Caco-2 and LogD as delivered, the network gains about as much as it does on QM9, so those gains are not evidence of noise. The support vector regressor, which also gives the same score on every fit, gains almost nothing from any cleanup on these labels. Label smoothing on hERG $K_i$ is the exception among the small molecules. Relative to its score, the network's first single representation gains several times what the same cleanup gives on QM9 (Table~\ref{tab:noise:res:shuffled}).

% TODO: manual review
QM9 also shows the limit of the shuffled-score control. With the network, label smoothing on QM9's first single representation beats all three shuffles, although there is no noise to find (Table~\ref{tab:noise:res:shuffled}). The shuffles themselves move about as much as the network does between fits. Passing the shuffled control with the network is therefore weak evidence by itself, and we read it beside QM9.

% TODO: manual review
Changing the model fails the test, so its gains are better training more than noise handling. On QM9 with no noise added, stacking the two models improves every configuration, and averaging network seeds improves most of them (Table~\ref{tab:noise:res:change_model_delivered}). On the labels as delivered of hERG $K_i$ and LogD the same changes help by about as much. Under injected Gaussian noise, seed averaging on hERG $K_i$ gains more than on the labels as delivered, in every configuration (Table~\ref{tab:noise:res:change_model}). Part of its gain under damage does respond to the noise.

% TODO: manual review
The twelve published methods pass the test in the other direction. On QM9 with no noise added each one made the network worse in the first run (Table~\ref{tab:noise:res:qm9_sweep}); in a second run about half beat the plain network, within the network's own spread. Where one of them helps a measured dataset as delivered, the gain is therefore more likely noise found. The heteroscedastic GP on $\Delta\Delta G$ is the clearest case: it raises the best single representation's Spearman and makes QM9 worse (Table~\ref{tab:noise:res:sweep_examples}).

% TODO: manual review
Noise is removed most clearly, then, where deletion or a published method helps an antibody dataset as delivered and does nothing for QM9. On the small-molecule labels as delivered only label smoothing on hERG $K_i$ clears the bar. The test rests on one computed property of one dataset, on the first outer fold, and several methods have no QM9 result yet (Section~\ref{sec:noise:limitations}).

