Two more experiments graded the plain residual under damage sized by the model's own error. It ranked the shifted molecules above chance on every dataset there, for example 0.20 to 0.25 against 0.106 on expression titer. Turning such a ranking into a decision is harder. Conformal $p$-values with a false-discovery-rate rule flagged no molecule at all at $q = 0.05$. At $q = 0.1$ they flagged none when the calibration set was drawn from the noisy training labels. Only with a clean calibration set, which a user would not have, did it flag any, 10 molecules in each of a few passes. Acting on those flags left the held-out score where it was in almost every pass.

