\section{Conclusions}\label{sec:noise:conclusion}

% TODO: manual review
On the first outer fold, we found noisy labels in QSAR data and, in several settings, corrected them. QM9, the one dataset with computed labels, gave the cleanest test of whether a method removes noise or trains a better model (Section~\ref{sec:noise:res:qm9test}). Deletion never helped QM9's clean labels, and the published methods did not help it beyond the network's own spread. Where they helped an antibody dataset as delivered, they found noise that was already in the data. Label smoothing and weighting improved QM9 as well, as did changes to the model. Their gains on measured labels as delivered are therefore largely better training, with label smoothing on hERG $K_i$ the exception. Corrections worked best with a flag: raising labels clipped at an assay limit won back half to three quarters of the lost RMSE on the small-molecule datasets, with no detector. With a detector, deleting or relabeling flagged molecules helped under label flips and outliers. These gains passed random and shuffled controls on hERG, hERG $K_i$, Buchwald--Hartwig and three antibody datasets, though QM9 shows that the shuffled control alone is weak evidence with the network. The hybrid stayed ahead of the single representation on small molecules in almost every condition, before and after treatment. Finding a detector's neighbors in the hybrid rather than in the model's own single representation helped slightly more often, with large differences in both directions. Which noise-handling method works best thus depends on the feature space it runs in.

