\chapter{Conclusions and Further Thoughts}\label{chap:discussion}

% TODO: manual review
This chapter answers the research questions of Chapter~\ref{chap:intro} on handling label noise in molecular data, then sets out how to choose a noise-handling method and what the thesis contributes.

\section{Summary of findings}\label{sec:discussion:summary}\label{sec:discussion:antibodies}

% TODO: manual review
The first half of the thesis asked four questions about label noise, answered in Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}. First, model family decided noise robustness more than the representation did. On QM9 under Gaussian noise, model family explained 47.2\% of the variance in \aucnorm{} and the representation far less, whereas the representation mattered more for predictive performance. Label noise corrupts the labels and leaves the features untouched, so this split is what one would expect. Second, random forests were the most consistent model under noise. LightGBM and XGBoost predicted well on clean labels and lost the most as noise rose, so a model's score on clean labels says little about how it holds up under noise. Third, the type of noise mattered more than the shape of its distribution. Systematic noise, shared within a scaffold family or clipped at an assay limit, did the most damage. A study that injects only Gaussian noise therefore misses it. Fourth, mean predicted uncertainty rose with noise, but no model's uncertainty identified the corrupted labels.

% TODO: manual review
Chapter~\ref{chap:flaws} asked why published methods for learning with noisy labels did poorly in an earlier exploratory study, and found that those comparing molecules by a distance did worst. Within one dataset, most features of a standard representation barely vary: on hERG $K_i$, 1,815 of the 2,048 ECFP4 features take one value in nearly every molecule. A distance sums over every feature, so these features dominate it. Any method that relies on such a distance inherits the problem, which the adaptive hybrid representation was built to repair.

% TODO: manual review
% SOURCE: /Volumes/seagate/kirby_results_2026-10-09/complexity/c02_complexity_matching_grid/<ds>.json (RERUN-SWAP-DNN)
The second half asked whether the representation can be adapted to the task. First, we found that the best set of features depends on the dataset and the downstream model, and a search can find it (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}). The adaptive hybrid representation beat the best single representation on every small-molecule dataset with both downstream models. The method carried over from small molecules to antibodies, although there the gain depended on the dataset. On two antibody datasets the best single representation was one built for this thesis, which reads only the regions or mutations that vary. Second, distance in a hybrid tracked the label better than in the single representation on all six small-molecule datasets, and predicting each label from its five nearest neighbors was more accurate (Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}). On antibodies and reactions, distance tracked the label better too. However, prediction from neighbors was worse on several of these datasets, so the benefit of a hybrid has to be measured on each one.

% TODO: manual review
Third, noisy labels could be found and corrected (Chapter~\ref{chap:noise}). With a quarter of LogD's training labels clipped, censored EM removed most of the error that clipping added to the neural network. Cleaning guided by a detector beat random deletion or shuffled detector scores on hERG, hERG $K_i$, Buchwald--Hartwig and three antibody datasets. On small molecules the hybrid stayed ahead of the single representation through every treatment.

% TODO: manual review
Fourth, feature importance helped in building the hybrid, but using the importance scores again during training added little. The hybrid ranks features by importance within each representation, and at the same number of features it beat recursive feature elimination and random features on all but one small-molecule dataset with a result. For finding noisy labels, the best feature space for the neighbor detectors depended on the dataset (Chapter~\ref{chap:noise}).

\section{Choosing a noise-handling method}\label{sec:discussion:noise}\label{sec:discussion:conditional}\label{sec:discussion:simple}

% TODO: manual review
No single method handled label noise on every dataset. The forest residual detector fell to chance under clipping, where acting on the known flag did best. Subtracting scaffold-family offsets made the model worse on Caco-2's labels as delivered, and deleting the molecules the network fits worst helped on the reactions but almost never on small molecules. Even the order of the methods changed between the hybrid and the single representations, so a method chosen on one representation may not be the best on another.

% TODO: manual review
We recommend modeling the noise in a dataset before choosing a method. Data pooled from several laboratories can carry an offset per source, and an assay with a reporting limit gives clipped labels; both come with a flag that a correction can use. A method that assumes noise that is not there can do harm. Simple methods were often as good as complex ones: random forests were the most consistent model, and on hERG $K_i$ one constant lift for every clipped label beat censored EM.

\section{Contributions}\label{sec:discussion:contributions}

% TODO: manual review
We make six contributions on label noise and three on the representation, in that order.
\begin{enumerate}
    \item NoiseInject, an open-source Python package for benchmarking noise robustness and uncertainty in QSAR models (Section~\ref{sec:noiseinject_pkg}).
    \item Noise conditions beyond Gaussian noise, including noise shared within scaffold families and labels clipped at an assay limit (Chapter~\ref{chap:background}).
    \item A split of the variance in performance and in noise robustness between model family and representation (Chapter~\ref{chap:robustness}).
    \item Published noise-handling methods adapted to QSAR data, for example censored EM with any downstream model, a random intercept per scaffold family, and the buddy check from meteorology as a detector of noisy labels (Chapter~\ref{chap:noise}).
    \item Three methods for which we found no earlier use: the cliff-aware gap, cleaning the labels before the hybrid search, and returning activity-cliff molecules after a cleanup (Chapter~\ref{chap:noise}).
    \item Tests of these methods on single representations and hybrids side by side, with three controls: QM9 with no noise added, random deletion and shuffled detector scores (Chapter~\ref{chap:noise}).
    \item The adaptive hybrid representation, which ranks features within each representation, splits a budget of features among them, and searches the selection settings for each dataset and model (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}). A newly published embedding is added to the search as one more representation.
    \item Hybrids built for distance (Chapter~\ref{chap:similarity}), and hybrids built from data held at separate sites (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}).
    \item Antibody representations built for this thesis: readouts of the complementarity-determining regions (CDRs) and of each region, mutation features, and the change in embedding from the parent antibody (Chapter~\ref{chap:method}).
\end{enumerate}

