\section{Future work}\label{sec:discussion:future}

% TODO: manual review
The hybrid search needs only several feature matrices for the same samples and a supervised task. Gene-expression profiles and financial time series are natural next domains, since each has many competing representations of the same samples. Runs on DNA promoter sequences have started but are not reported here. Beyond new domains, a hybrid can be the input to the model that scores molecules for a generative model; the code exists and has not been run. Starting the search from the settings found on a similar dataset may save compute, and a deployment across real sites would test the separate-site results of Chapter~\ref{chap:method}.

% TODO: manual review
The noise methods need more clean controls: QM9 offers twelve computed properties, and only the HOMO--LUMO gap was used. In QSAR, a class label usually comes from cutting a continuous measurement at a threshold, so measurement error flips mostly the molecules near that threshold. Flips of that kind, on more classification datasets, are the next test for the detectors.

\section{Concluding remarks}\label{sec:discussion:conclusions}\label{sec:discussion:claim}

% TODO: manual review
Label noise in molecular data can be handled. Which method handles it, however, depends on the prediction task and the data, as well as on the model and the kind of noise. No single model or representation was best on every dataset we tested, and neither was any noise-handling method. A dataset's noise should therefore be understood before a method is chosen.

% TODO: manual review
The features matter as well: when the noise methods failed, we followed the failure down to the distance between molecules and then to the features that distance is computed over. The adaptive hybrid representation selects those features for each dataset and model. On small molecules it predicted better than any single representation and gave better neighbors. It stayed ahead, moreover, through every noise treatment.

% TODO: manual review
A new foundation model appears every few months in molecular and protein modeling, antibodies included. With the hybrid search, a researcher does not need to choose between their current representation and a new one. They add the new embedding to the search and rerun it, and the search decides whether the embedding contributes features and which (tested on LogD and hERG blocking in Chapter~\ref{chap:method}). A choice usually made by habit becomes one measured on each task.

